var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#High-level-API-(not-exported)","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"Functions named like those of LinearAlgebra, owned by OxiBLAS and not exported: call them as OxiBLAS.lu, OxiBLAS.ldiv, etc. They are distinct from the functions of LinearAlgebra (OxiBLAS.lu !== LinearAlgebra.lu). The factorization types are subtypes of LinearAlgebra.Factorization.\n\nSources: src/api/.","title":"High-level API (not exported)"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS-methods-of-other-modules'-functions","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"These functions belong to LinearAlgebra, SparseArrays or Base; OxiBLAS adds methods for its own types to them (see the guides):\n\nLinearAlgebra.ldiv!, SparseArrays.blockdiag, SparseArrays.fkeep!","title":"OxiBLAS methods of other modules' functions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.BunchKaufman","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.BunchKaufman{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nSymmetric indefinite factorization with Bunch–Kaufman pivoting (1×1 and 2×2 blocks): A[p,p] = L·D·Lᴴ, or L·D·Lᵀ for a complex symmetric matrix. It wraps an engine of type E:\n\nLDLFactor (dense);\nSparseLDLFactor (sparse, with the MA57 threshold).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BunchKaufman"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.Cholesky","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.Cholesky{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nCholesky factorization A = L·Lᴴ, which wraps an engine of type E:\n\nCholeskyFactor (dense, lower triangle);\nMultifrontalCholeskyFactor (sparse: A[p,p] = L·Lᴴ).\n\nThe info field is nonzero when the factorization failed (check=false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.Cholesky"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.Eigen","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.Eigen{T,V,M,W} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nEigendecomposition A·vectors = vectors·Diagonal(values). It shares the engine's arrays without copying them.\n\nA Hermitian or real symmetric input goes to eigen_hermitian (real values, in ascending order).\nAny other input goes to eigen_general (complex values).\nvalues, vectors = F destructures it.\nIt provides no ldiv!: LinearAlgebra.Eigen has no solve either.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.Eigen"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.GeneralizedEigen","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.GeneralizedEigen{T,V,M,W} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nGeneralized eigendecomposition A·vectors = B·vectors·Diagonal(values). It shares the engine's arrays without copying them.\n\nHermitian/real symmetric wrappers go to eigen_generalized: Cholesky of B (which must be positive definite), then the Hermitian EVD, with real values and B-orthonormal vectors.\nAny other matrices go to eigen_pencil (QZ, then the pencil vectors), where values = α ./ β, possibly infinite.\nvalues, vectors = F destructures it. It provides no ldiv!, like LinearAlgebra.GeneralizedEigen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.GeneralizedEigen"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.GeneralizedSchur","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.GeneralizedSchur{T,C,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nGeneralized Schur (QZ) decomposition A = Q·S·Zᴴ, B = Q·T·Zᴴ (QZFactor). It has the properties S, T, Q, Z, α, β and values = α ./ β.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.GeneralizedSchur"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.Hessenberg","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.Hessenberg{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nHessenberg reduction A = Q·H·Qᴴ (HessenbergFactor).\n\nF.H is an UpperHessenberg matrix.\nF.Q is an implicit HessenbergQ.\nIt provides no ldiv!, since the engine has no shifted Hessenberg solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.Hessenberg"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.HessenbergQ","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.HessenbergQ{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.AbstractQ{T}\n\nThe Q factor of a Hessenberg reduction, applied through its reflectors (apply_hessenberg_q!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.HessenbergQ"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.LDLt","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.LDLt{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nLDLᵀ factorization without pivoting: A[p,p] = L·D·Lᴴ, with D diagonal and p a fill-reducing ordering (identity for a dense matrix). It wraps an engine of type E:\n\nLDLFactor (dense, pivot=:none);\nSparseLDLFactor (sparse, pivot=:none).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.LDLt"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.LQ","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.LQ{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nLQ factorization A = L·Q. It is stored in Householder form as the QR factorization of Aᴴ, which is what lq_factor computes; Q stays implicit. F \\ b returns the minimum-norm solution of a wide system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.LQ"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.LQPackedQ","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.LQPackedQ{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.AbstractQ{T}\n\nThe Q factor of an LQ factorization, which is the adjoint of the Q factor of the QR factorization of Aᴴ. Matrix(Q) gives the thin min(m,n)×n basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.LQPackedQ"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.LU","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.LU{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nLU factorization with row pivoting, which wraps an engine of type E:\n\nLUFactor (dense): F.L*F.U == A[F.p,:];\nMultifrontalLUFactor (sparse): F.L*F.U == A[F.p,F.q].\n\nThe type is mutable so that lu!(F, A) can refactorize in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.LU"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.QR","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.QR{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nHouseholder QR factorization A = Q·R (QRFactor). F.Q is an implicit AbstractQ, and F \\ b returns the least-squares solution for a tall A and the minimum-norm solution for a wide one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.QR"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.QRPackedQ","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.QRPackedQ{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.AbstractQ{T}\n\nThe Q factor of a QR factorization, applied through its Householder reflectors and never formed. Matrix(Q) gives the thin m×min(m,n) basis, as for LinearAlgebra's QR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.QRPackedQ"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.QRPivoted","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.QRPivoted{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nQR factorization with column pivoting, A[:,p] = Q·R (PivotedQRFactor).\n\nThe solution is the minimum-norm one: the numerical rank is read from the diagonal of R, with |Rᵢᵢ| > min(m,n)·eps·|R₁₁|.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.QRPivoted"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.QRSparse","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.QRSparse{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nMultifrontal sparse QR factorization (SparseQRFactor) with the SPQR contract Q*R == A[prow,pcol]. The engine folds its row reordering into Q (transpositions stored as reflectors), so prow is the identity. Its methods are in linalgapisparse.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.QRSparse"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.SVD","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.SVD{T,Tr,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nSingular value decomposition A = U·Diagonal(S)·Vt (SVDFactor).\n\nF \\ b returns the minimum-norm least-squares solution. Singular values at or below eps·S[1] are dropped, as in LinearAlgebra.\nU, S, V = F destructures the factorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SVD"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.Schur","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.Schur{T,C,E} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nSchur decomposition A = Z·T·Zᴴ (SchurFactor). For real input, T is real quasi-triangular. It has the properties T, Z (also vectors) and values, and destructures as T, Z, values = F.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.Schur"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.SparseQRQ","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.SparseQRQ{T,E} <: LinearAlgebra.AbstractQ{T}\n\nThe Q factor of a sparse QR factorization, which is a list of sparse reflectors (sparse_apply_q!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseQRQ"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.TriangularEngine","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.TriangularEngine{T,S,K}\n\nEngine of an OxiBLAS.LU for a matrix that is already triangular: dense (UpperTriangular…), bidiagonal, diagonal, triangular BandedMatrix or triangular PackedMatrix.\n\nSolves are substitutions with the matching kernel: trsm!/trsv! (dense), tbsv! (band; a bidiagonal is stored as a 2×n band), tpsv! (packed), or a division (diagonal). They cost O(n²) for dense storage and O(n·k) for a band.\nFor an upper (or diagonal) matrix, it is the partial-pivoting LU (L = I, p = identity).\nFor a lower matrix, it is the LU without pivoting, L = A·D⁻¹ and U = D. It is used only by factorize, since lu (RowMaximum) has to pivot.\nsrc is the engine's own copy of the input, and kernel the storage handed to the kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.TriangularEngine"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.bunchkaufman","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.bunchkaufman(A, rook=false; check=true) -> OxiBLAS.BunchKaufman\n\nBunch–Kaufman factorization of a symmetric or Hermitian matrix (ldlt_factor, pivot=:bunchkaufman).\n\nOnly rook=false is available: rook pivoting has no engine.\nA singular matrix throws SingularException whatever the value of check, because the engine reports it while factorizing.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.bunchkaufman(A::sparse, rook=false; check=true, perm=nothing) -> OxiBLAS.BunchKaufman\n\nMultifrontal LDLᵀ factorization with 1×1 and 2×2 Bunch–Kaufman pivoting inside the fronts and the MA57 threshold (sparse_ldlt). perm supplies a custom fill-reducing permutation vector (nothing uses AMD). Only rook=false is supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bunchkaufman"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.cholesky","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.cholesky(A, [NoPivot()]; check=true) -> OxiBLAS.Cholesky\n\nCholesky factorization of a Hermitian positive definite matrix (cholesky_factor).\n\nAccepts Hermitian, real Symmetric and plain matrices; a plain matrix is checked for being Hermitian.\ncheck=false returns a factorization whose issuccess is false instead of throwing PosDefException.\ncholesky!(A) factors in place: the lower triangle of A (a Matrix or a strided view of floating type) is overwritten by L, its strict upper triangle is zeroed, and A becomes F.factors. For a wrapper with uplo='U', the conjugated upper triangle is first mirrored into the lower one inside the parent.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.cholesky(A::SymTridiagonal{<:Real} / BandedMatrix / PackedMatrix / Diagonal; check=true)\n\nCholesky factorization in the storage of A:\n\nband (band_cholesky, O(n·kd²)): F.L is a lower triangular BandedMatrix;\npacked (packed_cholesky): F.L is a triangular PackedMatrix;\ndiagonal: F.L is a Diagonal.\n\nA must be symmetric (real) or Hermitian in its own storage; a general or triangular BandedMatrix/PackedMatrix gets an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.cholesky / ldlt / bunchkaufman / lu / factorize / det / isposdef\n    on Hermitian(S) or Symmetric(S), with S a Diagonal, Bidiagonal,\n    Tridiagonal or SymTridiagonal\n\nGo through the triangle that the wrapper selects, converted in O(n) to a symmetric or Hermitian BandedMatrix, so to the band engines (band_cholesky, multifrontal LDLᵀ, band_lu). For a real symmetric input, eigen/eigvals go to eigen_tridiagonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.cholesky!","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.cholesky!(F::OxiBLAS.Cholesky, A; check=true, shift=0) -> F\n\nRefactorizes A + shift*I, which has the same pattern, into F. The ordering F.p is reused, so the AMD step is skipped; the rest of the symbolic analysis is redone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.cholesky!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.cond","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.cond(A, p=2)\n\nCondition number of A in the 1-, 2- or ∞-norm (condition_number).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.cond"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.det","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.det(A), OxiBLAS.logdet(A), OxiBLAS.logabsdet(A)\n\nDeterminant functions computed from an LU factorization. On an OxiBLAS factorization, they forward to LinearAlgebra's generic methods, which use the logabsdet primitive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.det"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.eigen","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.eigen(A, B) -> OxiBLAS.GeneralizedEigen\nOxiBLAS.eigvals(A, B)\n\nGeneralized eigenproblem A·x = λ·B·x.\n\nHermitian/real Symmetric wrappers: eigen_generalized, which raises PosDefException if B is not positive definite, as in LinearAlgebra.\nAny other matrices: eigen_pencil (QZ).\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.eigen(A::SymTridiagonal{<:Real}) / eigvals\n\nImplicit-QR symmetric tridiagonal eigensolver (eigen_tridiagonal), with ascending values and O(n) memory without vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.eigen(D::Diagonal; sortby) / eigvals\n\nThe values are the diagonal, sorted as in LinearAlgebra (real part, then imaginary part); the vectors are the matching permutation of the identity. With sortby=nothing, the values stay unsorted and the vectors are a Diagonal (O(n)).\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.eigen(A::UpperTriangular / UnitUpperTriangular)\n\nThe values are the diagonal; the vectors come from back substitution on the triangular matrix (trevc_right), with no QR iteration. Not available for a lower triangular matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.eigmax","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.eigmax(A), OxiBLAS.eigmin(A)\n\nLargest or smallest eigenvalue, from OxiBLAS.eigvals, which picks the structured engine when there is one. The engines may return complex values whose imaginary parts are exactly zero; those are compared by their real parts. A DomainError is raised only when some eigenvalue really is complex (nonzero imaginary part), which is LinearAlgebra's contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigmax"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.exp","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.exp(A), OxiBLAS.log(A), OxiBLAS.sqrt(A), OxiBLAS.sin(A), OxiBLAS.cos(A)\n\nMatrix functions on the native engines:\n\nmatrix_exp: Padé scaling and squaring;\nmatrix_log: complex Schur, then inverse scaling and squaring;\nmatrix_sqrt: complex Schur, then triangular recurrence;\nmatrix_sin, matrix_cos: through exp(±iA).\n\nOn a number they are Base's functions. No other type falls back to Base/LinearAlgebra.\n\nThe engines exploit no structure: they make one working copy of any AbstractMatrix, which is passed to them as is. The result keeps the structure that f preserves:\n\nInput Result\nDiagonal Diagonal, entry by entry (O(n))\nupper/lower triangular (dense, Bidiagonal, triangular band/packed) UpperTriangular/LowerTriangular\nHermitian (wrapper, Hermitian band/packed) exp/sin/cos: Hermitian. log/sqrt: Hermitian eigendecomposition V·f(λ)·Vᴴ, as in LinearAlgebra; Hermitian if A is positive definite (log) or semidefinite (sqrt, every λ ≥ -n·eps·max abs(λ)), otherwise a full complex matrix with the branch f(complex(λ)), never forced Hermitian\nsymmetric (Symmetric, SymTridiagonal, symmetric band/packed) Symmetric (f(A)ᵀ = f(Aᵀ))\nTridiagonal, general BandedMatrix, other full matrix (dense in general)\n\nThe imaginary part is dropped only when the result is real mathematically:\n\nsin and cos of a real matrix;\nlog of a real symmetric positive definite matrix, sqrt of a real symmetric positive semidefinite one;\nlog/sqrt of a real triangular matrix with a positive diagonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.exp"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.factorize","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.factorize(A)\n\nChooses a factorization from the structure of A:\n\nsquare, Hermitian: Cholesky if it is positive definite, otherwise BunchKaufman;\nother square matrices: LU;\nrectangular: QRPivoted.\n\nTriangular and diagonal structures are not detected. Unlike LinearAlgebra.factorize, the result is never a LinearAlgebra wrapper, whose solves would go through BLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.factorize(A::sparse)\n\nChooses a sparse factorization from the structure of A, in the order of SparseArrays' factorize and \\:\n\ntriangular (or diagonal): LU with a TriangularEngine, the native sparse substitution (tested in O(nnz));\nHermitian: Cholesky, or BunchKaufman if A is not positive definite;\ncomplex Symmetric: BunchKaufman (complex symmetric LDLᵀ);\nother square matrices: LU;\nrectangular: QRSparse (it solves the tall case only).\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.factorize(UpperTriangular(A::sparse)) (and the lower/unit wrappers)\n\nLU with a TriangularEngine over the native sparse substitution (sparse_triangular_solve): no elimination, and only the wrapped triangle is read. The engine keeps its own copy of A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.factorize"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.hessenberg","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.hessenberg(A) -> OxiBLAS.Hessenberg\n\nHessenberg reduction (hessenberg_factor). hessenberg! does not overwrite A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hessenberg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.inv","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.inv(A)\n\nInverse of a square matrix (native_inverse). On an OxiBLAS factorization, it uses LinearAlgebra's generic inv, built on ldiv!. Sparse matrices are rejected, as in SparseArrays.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.inv(A) for structured matrices\n\nDiagonal → Diagonal (O(n)).\nTriangular → the same triangular wrapper, from the substitution solves of the identity.\nAny other structure → the dense inverse from its structured factorization (the inverse of a band matrix is dense).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.inv"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.isposdef","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.isposdef(A)\n\nWhether A is Hermitian positive definite, decided by an attempted Cholesky factorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.isposdef"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.kron","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.kron(A, B)\n\nKronecker product (kron_product), with the element types promoted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.kron"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.ldiv","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.ldiv(F::Factorization, B) -> X\nOxiBLAS.ldiv(A::AbstractMatrix, B) -> X\n\nAllocating form of OxiBLAS.ldiv!. X is a Vector or a Matrix, like B, with element type promote_op(\\, eltype(F), eltype(B)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ldiv"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.ldiv!","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, F::Factorization, B; workspace=nothing) -> Y\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, A::AbstractMatrix, B; workspace=nothing) -> Y\n\nSolves A*X = B and writes X into Y, which has size(A, 2) rows and as many columns as B.\n\nF is any factorization with a LinearAlgebra.ldiv!(F, X) method: the OxiBLAS ones, and their transpose/adjoint. It is reused as it is.\nA is factorized first with OxiBLAS.factorize (dense, structured or sparse input). An input that it does not accept gets a MethodError.\nB is never modified, except with Y === B on a square system, which solves in place. A Y that only partially overlaps B is rejected.\nA tall F (m > n) gives the least-squares solution, a wide one (n > m) the solution of that factorization's own ldiv! (the minimum-norm one for dense QR/LQ); the sparse QR has no wide solve.\nworkspace (OxiBLAS extension beyond LinearAlgebra.ldiv!).\nWhy: For a tall orthogonal factorization (QR, QRPivoted, QRSparse, or LQ' with m > n), the in-place primitive LinearAlgebra.ldiv!(F, X) applies Qᴴ to an m-row buffer before extracting the n solution rows. Because Y only has n < m rows and B must not be overwritten, an m-row working buffer X is required.\nWhen you do NOT need it: On square systems (m == n), wide systems (n > m, where Y itself has n > m rows and serves as the working buffer), SVD factorizations (which project into rank-sized scratch and write directly into Y), or when B is sparse (whose dense copy is reused in place), OxiBLAS.ldiv! already avoids allocating X with workspace=nothing.\nWhen and how to use it: When solving repeated tall least-squares systems (m > n) in a hot loop with a dense B and QR / QRPivoted / QRSparse / LQ', preallocate ws = Vector{T}(undef, m) (or Matrix{T}(undef, m, nrhs)) once per task and pass workspace=ws to eliminate the m-row buffer allocation. workspace must have at least (max(m, n), size(B, 2)) elements, 1-based indexing, must not alias Y or B, and must be exclusive to the calling task during the call.\nY must be a dense array: a solution is dense. A sparse B is read through a dense copy.\nThe solve runs in the factor's element type. A real factor and a complex B of the same precision are solved as two real systems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ldiv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.ldlt","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.ldlt(A) -> OxiBLAS.LDLt\n\nLDLᵀ factorization without pivoting of a symmetric or Hermitian matrix. LinearAlgebra only provides it for SymTridiagonal (dense) and CHOLMOD (sparse). It throws on a zero pivot; prefer bunchkaufman for indefinite matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.ldlt(A::sparse; perm=nothing, shift=0) -> OxiBLAS.LDLt\n\nMultifrontal LDLᵀ factorization without pivoting (sparse_ldlt, pivot=:none), with an AMD ordering or the permutation perm and optional diagonal shift shift (A + shift*I), as in CHOLMOD's ldlt.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.ldlt(A::SymTridiagonal / symmetric BandedMatrix)\nOxiBLAS.bunchkaufman(A::SymTridiagonal / symmetric BandedMatrix)\n\nLDLᵀ without pivoting (as LinearAlgebra's ldlt(::SymTridiagonal)), or with Bunch–Kaufman pivoting. Both use the multifrontal engine (sparse_ldlt) in natural order, in O(n·kd²) time and memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ldlt"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.ldlt!","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.ldlt!(F, A; shift=0) -> F\nOxiBLAS.bunchkaufman!(F, A) -> F\n\nRefactorizes A (or A + shift*I for ldlt!), which has the same pattern, into F, reusing the ordering F.p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ldlt!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.lq","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.lq(A) -> OxiBLAS.LQ\n\nLQ factorization, stored as the QR factorization of Aᴴ (qr_factor), as lq_factor computes it. lq! does not overwrite A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lq"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.lu","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.lu(A, [RowMaximum()]; check=true) -> OxiBLAS.LU\n\nLU factorization with partial pivoting (lu_factor). This function is not LinearAlgebra.lu.\n\ncheck=false returns a factorization whose issuccess is false when A is singular.\nlu!(A) factors in place: A (a Matrix or a strided view of floating type) is overwritten by the factors and becomes F.factors, with no copy.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.lu(A::SparseMatrixCSC / CSRMatrix / ...; check=true) -> OxiBLAS.LU\n\nSparse LU factorization with threshold pivoting (multifrontal_lu, ordering=:auto), giving F.L*F.U == A[F.p,F.q]. A singular matrix throws SingularException whatever the value of check: the engine has no failure state to return.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.lu(A::Tridiagonal / SymTridiagonal / Bidiagonal / Diagonal / BandedMatrix)\n\nBand LU with partial pivoting (band_lu). It works in (2kl+ku+1)×n compact storage, which includes the fill from pivoting, in O(n·kl·(kl+ku)) time (O(n) for a tridiagonal).\n\nF.L, F.U (sparse) and F.p are rebuilt on demand so that F.L*F.U == A[F.p,:].\nA singular matrix throws SingularException whatever the value of check.\nThere is no lu!: band storage cannot hold the fill.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.lu(A::UpperTriangular / UnitUpperTriangular / packed upper triangular)\n\nThe partial-pivoting LU of an upper triangular matrix is L = I, U = A. It is returned without elimination, on a copy, with substitution solves (TriangularEngine). A lower triangular matrix may need row swaps, so a dense one goes through the dense LU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lu"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.lu!","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.lu!(F::OxiBLAS.LU, A) -> F\n\nRefactorizes A into F. The engine has no separate symbolic phase to reuse, so the analysis is redone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lu!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.lyap","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.lyap(A, C)\n\nSolution X of A·X + X·Aᴴ + C = 0 (continuous Lyapunov equation), as sylvester(A, Aᴴ, C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lyap"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.mul","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.mul(A, B)\n\nAllocating product. The result keeps structure when it is cheap and exact: sparse × sparse is sparse (SparseMatrixCSC/SparseVector when a SparseArrays operand is involved, CSRMatrix otherwise), Diagonal × Diagonal is Diagonal, Diagonal scaling of a sparse matrix stays sparse, a product of two upper (lower) triangular matrices is UpperTriangular (LowerTriangular); anything else is a dense Array of the promoted element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.mul"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.mul!","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.mul!(C, A, B, α=true, β=false)\n\nC ← α·A·B + β·C with OxiBLAS kernels, for dense matrices and vectors (and their transposes/adjoints and views), LinearAlgebra structured matrices (Diagonal, triangular, Symmetric/Hermitian, Bidiagonal/Tridiagonal/ SymTridiagonal), SparseArrays matrices and vectors, OxiBLAS sparse formats and 2-D SparseTensors. C may be dense, Diagonal or sparse (SparseMatrixCSC, SparseVector, CSRMatrix, CSCMatrix, SparseTensor); a sparse destination receives the exact pattern of the result. β=0 never reads C. Operands whose element type differs from C's are converted, keeping their structure and storage format. Triangular/Symmetric/Hermitian wrappers of a sparse matrix are handled as the sparse matrix they denote (never densified).\n\nAliasing: an operand whose storage overlaps C (the same array, overlapping views, a Diagonal or structured wrapper of such data) is copied first, so the result is always that of non-overlapping operands.\n\nA sparse destination is filled from a temporary product: sparse when both operands are sparse, dense when one of them is dense (that product is dense in general anyway).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.mul!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.norm","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.norm(A, p=2)\n\nNorm of the entries, as in LinearAlgebra.\n\nOn a matrix: p=2 is the Frobenius norm (matrix_norm(:fro)), 1 the sum of moduli, Inf the largest modulus.\nOn a vector: p=2 uses nrm2.\n\nOther p values use the generic sum (sum |x|ᵖ)^(1/p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.norm"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.nullspace","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.nullspace(A; atol=0, rtol=...)\n\nOrthonormal basis of the null space of A (null_space).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.nullspace"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.opnorm","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.opnorm(A, p=2)\n\nOperator norm (matrix_norm): 1 (column sums), 2 (largest singular value) or Inf (row sums).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.opnorm"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.ordschur","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.ordschur(F::OxiBLAS.Schur, select) / ordschur!(F, select)\nOxiBLAS.ordschur(F::OxiBLAS.GeneralizedSchur, select) / ordschur!(F, select)\n\nReorders the (generalized) Schur form so that the selected eigenvalues come first: schur_reorder (unitary swaps), or qz_reorder for QZ.\n\nselect is a Bool vector in the order of F.values, as in LinearAlgebra. Selecting one value of a real conjugate pair selects the whole 2×2 block.\nordschur! replaces the factors inside F and returns F; the original arrays are not overwritten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ordschur"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.pinv","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.pinv(A; atol=0, rtol=...)\n\nMoore–Penrose pseudoinverse of A (pseudoinverse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.pinv"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.qr","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.qr(A, [NoPivot() | ColumnNorm()]) -> OxiBLAS.QR or OxiBLAS.QRPivoted\n\nHouseholder QR factorization (qr_factor). With ColumnNorm(), the columns are pivoted (qr_pivoted). qr! does not overwrite A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qr"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.rank","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.rank(A; atol=0, rtol=...)\n\nNumerical rank of A from its singular values (numerical_rank).\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.rank(A), OxiBLAS.cond(A) for `Diagonal` and `Bidiagonal`\n\nComputed from the structured singular values (svdvals): O(n log n) for Diagonal, O(n) memory for Bidiagonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.rank"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.schur","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.schur(A) -> OxiBLAS.Schur\nOxiBLAS.schur(A, B) -> OxiBLAS.GeneralizedSchur\n\nSchur decomposition (schur_factor), or generalized Schur decomposition by QZ (qz_factor). schur! does not overwrite its arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.schur"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.sparsevec","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.sparsevec(I, V, n=maximum(I); combine=+) -> SparseTensor{T,1}\nOxiBLAS.sparsevec(v::AbstractVector) -> SparseTensor{T,1}\n\nOxiBLAS sparse vector, as SparseArrays.sparsevec:\n\nfrom indices and values, with duplicates combined by combine;\nfrom a dense vector, keeping its nonzero entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparsevec"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.spdiagm","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.spdiagm(k₁ => v₁, k₂ => v₂, ...) -> CSCMatrix\nOxiBLAS.spdiagm(m, n, k₁ => v₁, ...)\nOxiBLAS.spdiagm(v) -> CSCMatrix\n\nSparse matrix with the vectors vᵢ on the diagonals kᵢ (k > 0 above the main diagonal), as SparseArrays.spdiagm. The default size is the smallest square that holds every diagonal; duplicate positions are summed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.spdiagm"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.svd","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.svd(A; full=false) -> OxiBLAS.SVD\n\nSingular value decomposition (svd_factor). svd! does not overwrite A.\n\n\n\n\n\nOxiBLAS.svd(B::Bidiagonal) / svdvals(B::Bidiagonal)\nOxiBLAS.svd(D::Diagonal) / svdvals(D::Diagonal)\n\nBidiagonal: Golub–Kahan QR (the svd_bidiagonal kernel) applied directly to (|d|,|e|) after removing the phases, with no Householder reduction and no dense copy. Diagonal: sorted moduli, with vectors made of the phases and the permutation, as in LinearAlgebra.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#OxiBLAS.sylvester","page":"High-level API (not exported)","text":"OxiBLAS.sylvester(A, B, C)\n\nSolution X of A·X + X·B + C = 0, LinearAlgebra's convention. Bartels–Stewart on the native engines:\n\nSchur forms A = U·S·Uᴴ and B = V·T·Vᴴ (schur_factor).\nS·Y + Y·T = -Uᴴ·C·V, solved by generalized_sylvester (TGSYL) with the pencils (S, I) and (-T, I) and a zero second right-hand side, which forces R = L.\nX = U·Y·Vᴴ.\n\nThe equation has a unique solution only when no λᵢ(A) + μⱼ(B) is zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sylvester"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/factorizations/#Dense-factorizations-and-LAPACK","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"The dense engines are written in Julia and reuse the native GEMM for their blocked updates. Each one returns its own object (LUFactor, QRFactor…), usable with solve or through the high-level API, which wraps it in a LinearAlgebra.Factorization.\n\nusing OxiBLAS, LinearAlgebra\nA = randn(80, 80) + 80I; b = randn(80)\nF = lu_factor(A)                 # F.L*F.U ≈ A[F.p,:]\nx = solve(F, b)\nH = Hermitian(A * A')\nC = cholesky_factor(Matrix(H))   # lower triangle, F.L*F.L' ≈ A\nQ = qr_factor(randn(100, 40))    # least squares: least_squares(Q, y)\nnorm(A * x - b) / norm(b)\n4.3816175785436744e-16","title":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/factorizations/#Linear-systems","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Function Result\nlu_factor(A; blocksize, check, threaded, pivot=:partial) blocked LU; pivot=:complete or :rook (lu_complete, lu_rook)\ncholesky_factor(A; blocksize, threaded) Cholesky, lower triangle\nldlt_factor(A; uplo, hermitian, pivot=:bunchkaufman), bunchkaufman_factor 1×1/2×2 D blocks, A[p,p] ≈ L·D·Lᴴ; pivot=:none possible\naasen_factor Aasen factorization\nsolve(F, b), solve_transposed solve with a factorization\nleast_squares(A, b) least squares, m ≥ n, full rank\nmixed_solve(A, b) LU in reduced precision, Float64 refinement\nexpert_solve, refine_solve, expert_cholesky, expert_symmetric equilibration, iterative refinement, backward error\nnative_inverse, cholesky_inverse, pseudoinverse inverses\ndeterminant, logabsdet_factor, condition_number, reciprocal_condition, inverse_norm_estimate determinant and conditioning","title":"Linear systems"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/factorizations/#Orthogonalizations-and-rank","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Function Result\nqr_factor(A; blocksize, threaded, pivot=false), qr_pivoted compact WY QR; column pivoting A[:,F.p] ≈ F.Q*F.R\nlq_factor, ql_factor, rq_factor, complete_orthogonal other orthogonal decompositions\napply_q!, qr_q apply or form Q\nnumerical_rank, null_space, left_null_space, column_space, row_space rank and subspaces","title":"Orthogonalizations and rank"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/factorizations/#Eigenvalues-and-singular-values","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Function Result\nhessenberg_factor, hessenberg_q, apply_hessenberg_q! Hessenberg reduction\nschur_factor, schur_multishift, eigenvalues, eigen_general Schur (shifted QR), general eigenvalues and eigenvectors\nschur_reorder, schur_eigenvectors, trevc_right, trevc_left, trsna_s, trsna_sep, schur_condition reordering, vectors, conditioning\ntridiagonal_factor, eigen_tridiagonal, eigen_tridiagonal_dc, eigen_tridiagonal_mrrr Hermitian tridiagonal (implicit QR, divide and conquer, MRRR)\neigen_hermitian, eigen_hermitian_dc, eigen_hermitian_mrrr, eigen_generalized standard and generalized Hermitian problems\nqz_factor, eigen_pencil, qz_reorder, qz_condition, generalized_sylvester matrix pencils (real or complex QZ)\nsvd_factor(A; algorithm), singular_values SVD: one-sided Jacobi (default) or :golub_kahan\nsvd_bidiagonal, svd_divide_conquer, svd_truncated, svd_selected, svd_randomized variants and partial SVDs\nbidiagonal_factor, balance_matrix, balance_backtransform auxiliary reductions\n\nSVD. The default Jacobi guarantees relative accuracy on small singular values, at several times the cost of gesdd. algorithm=:golub_kahan is the fast path (see the high-level suite in Performance).","title":"Eigenvalues and singular values"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/factorizations/#Matrix-functions","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"matrix_exp, matrix_log, matrix_sqrt, matrix_power, matrix_sign, matrix_sin, matrix_cos, the Fréchet derivatives (frechet_exp, frechet_log, frechet_sqrt, frechet_matrix), matrix_norm, kron_product, khatri_rao, kron_sum and kronecker_action. The high-level versions OxiBLAS.exp, log, sqrt, sin and cos keep the structure when it is guaranteed (see the high-level API).","title":"Matrix functions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/factorizations/#Extended-precision","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"The generic algorithms accept other floating-point types (BigFloat, Float16, extended-precision types provided by other packages), as do gemm_extended! and dot_extended.","title":"Extended precision"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/factorizations/#Errors","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"SingularException and PosDefException are those of LinearAlgebra (re-exported under the same names). ConvergenceError reports that an iteration limit was reached.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/structured/#Structured-matrices-(packed-and-banded)","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"Sources: src/structured/.","title":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.AasenFactor","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"AasenFactor{T}\n\nResult of aasen_factor: symmetric indefinite factorization A[p,p] = L·Tri·Lᴴ with Tri tridiagonal (Aasen's method). Solve with solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.AasenFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.BandCholeskyFactor","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"BandCholeskyFactor{T}\n\nResult of band_cholesky: Cholesky factor in band storage (kd off-diagonals). Solve with solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BandCholeskyFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.BandLUFactor","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"BandLUFactor{T}\n\nResult of band_lu: LU with partial pivoting in band storage (the upper band grows by kl for the pivots). Solve with solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BandLUFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.BandedMatrix","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"BLAS band storage: general A[i,j]=data[ku+1+i-j,j].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BandedMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.PackedCholeskyFactor","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"PackedCholeskyFactor{T}\n\nResult of packed_cholesky: the lower Cholesky factor in packed storage (n(n+1)/2 entries). Solve with solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.PackedCholeskyFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.PackedMatrix","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"PackedMatrix(data, n; uplo=:U, kind=:symmetric, unit=false)\n\nBLAS packed storage of a symmetric, Hermitian or triangular matrix of order n: the uplo triangle stored column by column in data (length n(n+1)/2). kind is :symmetric, :hermitian or :triangular (unit: implicit unit diagonal). Built from a dense matrix with pack_matrix; used by spmv!, tpsv!, packed_cholesky and the high-level API (OxiBLAS.cholesky, \\).\n\nP = pack_matrix(H; uplo=:U, kind=:symmetric)\ny = zeros(n); spmv!(y, P, x)          # y = H*x\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.PackedMatrix"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.aasen_factor","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"Partially pivoted Aasen tridiagonal congruence A[p,p]=LTL' (or LTL^T). The first column of the unit lower L stays e1. Only uplo is read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.aasen_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.band_lu","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"Partial-pivoted band LU in (2kl+ku+1)×n compact storage. Local pivot operations and multipliers are retained in elimination order, as in GBTRF. No full matrix expansion is made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.band_lu"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.band_matrix","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"band_matrix(A; kl=0, ku=0, kind=:general, uplo=:U, unit=false) -> BandedMatrix\n\nCopies the band of A into a BandedMatrix: kl subdiagonals and ku superdiagonals for kind=:general; for :symmetric, :hermitian and :triangular the uplo triangle with max(kl, ku) off-diagonals. Only the band is read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.band_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.gbmv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"gbmv!(y, P::BandedMatrix, x, α=1, β=0; trans=:N, kernel=:auto, threaded=true) -> y\n\nGeneral banded matrix–vector product y ← α·op(P)·x + β·y (BLAS gbmv), only the band is read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gbmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.hbmv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"hbmv!(y, P::BandedMatrix, x, α=1, β=0; kernel=:auto, threaded=true) -> y\n\nHermitian banded matrix–vector product (BLAS hbmv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hbmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.hpmv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"hpmv!(y, P::PackedMatrix, x, α=1, β=0; kernel=:auto, threaded=true) -> y\n\nHermitian packed matrix–vector product (BLAS hpmv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hpmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.hpr!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"hpr!(P::PackedMatrix, x, α=1) -> P\n\nHermitian packed rank-1 update P ← α·x·xᴴ + P, α real (BLAS hpr).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hpr!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.hpr2!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"hpr2!(P::PackedMatrix, x, y, α=1) -> P\n\nHermitian packed rank-2 update P ← α·x·yᴴ + conj(α)·y·xᴴ + P (BLAS hpr2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hpr2!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.pack_matrix","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"pack_matrix(A; uplo=:U, kind=:symmetric, unit=false) -> PackedMatrix\n\nCopies the uplo triangle of A into a PackedMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.pack_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.packed_cholesky","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"Native packed Cholesky, retaining only n(n+1)/2 lower entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.packed_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.sbmv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"sbmv!(y, P::BandedMatrix, x, α=1, β=0; kernel=:auto, threaded=true) -> y\n\nSymmetric banded matrix–vector product (BLAS sbmv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sbmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.spmv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"spmv!(y, P::PackedMatrix, x, α=1, β=0; kernel=:auto, threaded=true) -> y\n\nSymmetric packed matrix–vector product y ← α·P·x + β·y (BLAS spmv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.spmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.spr!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"spr!(P::PackedMatrix, x, α=1) -> P\n\nSymmetric packed rank-1 update P ← α·x·xᵀ + P (BLAS spr).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.spr!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.spr2!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"spr2!(P::PackedMatrix, x, y, α=1) -> P\n\nSymmetric packed rank-2 update P ← α·x·yᵀ + α·y·xᵀ + P (BLAS spr2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.spr2!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.tbmv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"tbmv!(x, P::BandedMatrix; trans=:N, kernel=:auto) -> x\n\nTriangular banded matrix–vector product x ← op(P)·x (BLAS tbmv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tbmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.tbsv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"tbsv!(x, P::BandedMatrix; trans=:N, kernel=:auto) -> x\n\nTriangular banded solve op(P)·x = b in place (BLAS tbsv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tbsv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.tpmv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"tpmv!(x, P::PackedMatrix; trans=:N, kernel=:auto) -> x\n\nTriangular packed matrix–vector product x ← op(P)·x (BLAS tpmv); P must have kind=:triangular.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tpmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/structured/#OxiBLAS.tpsv!","page":"Structured matrices (packed and banded)","text":"tpsv!(x, P::PackedMatrix; trans=:N, kernel=:auto) -> x\n\nTriangular packed solve op(P)·x = b in place (BLAS tpsv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tpsv!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/structured/#Structured-matrices","page":"Structured matrices","text":"OxiBLAS provides two compact storages and supports the structured types of LinearAlgebra through its high-level API.","title":"Structured matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/structured/#Packed-and-banded-storage","page":"Structured matrices","text":"PackedMatrix(data, n; uplo, kind, unit). BLAS packed storage, column by column. pack_matrix(A; ...) builds it from a matrix; kind is :symmetric, :hermitian or :triangular.\nBandedMatrix(data, m; kl, ku, kind=:general, uplo, unit). BLAS band storage, data[ku+1+i-j, j]; band_matrix(A; ...) copies only the band.\n\nusing OxiBLAS, LinearAlgebra\nA = randn(6, 6); H = A * A' + 6I\nP = pack_matrix(H; uplo=:U, kind=:symmetric)\nx = randn(6); y = zeros(6)\nspmv!(y, P, x)                 # y ← P·x\ny ≈ H * x\ntrue\n\nOperations Functions\npacked products spmv!, hpmv!, tpmv!, tpsv!, spr!, spr2!, hpr!, hpr2!\nbanded products gbmv!, sbmv!, hbmv!, tbmv!, tbsv!\nfactorizations band_lu, band_cholesky, packed_cholesky (BandLUFactor, BandCholeskyFactor, PackedCholeskyFactor)\n\nThese operations only visit the stored coefficients. They accept kernel=:auto or the ISA requests of GEMM; :scalar selects the generic loop.","title":"Packed and banded storage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/structured/#Structured-types-of-LinearAlgebra","page":"Structured matrices","text":"OxiBLAS.lu, cholesky, ldlt, bunchkaufman, factorize, det, inv, eigen, svd… accept Diagonal, Bidiagonal, Tridiagonal, SymTridiagonal, the triangular matrices and their Hermitian/Symmetric wrappers. The chosen engine exploits the structure.\n\nType Engine\nTridiagonal band_lu, O(n)\nreal SymTridiagonal band_cholesky (cholesky), eigen_tridiagonal (eigen)\nBidiagonal substitution (factorize), bidiagonal QR kernel (svd)\nDiagonal direct operations, O(n)\ntriangular TriangularEngine: substitution, no elimination\nBandedMatrix band_lu, band_cholesky, multifrontal for LDLᵀ\nPackedMatrix packed_cholesky, tpsv!\n\nThe LinearAlgebra functions are left intact. The structured types of Julia only receive OxiBLAS.* methods: LinearAlgebra.lu(::Tridiagonal) stays the one of Julia. Only BandedMatrix and PackedMatrix, which are OxiBLAS types, extend LinearAlgebra functions.","title":"Structured types of LinearAlgebra"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/structured/#Limitations","page":"Structured matrices","text":"No structured engine for QR, LQ, Schur, Hessenberg and the non-symmetric spectra of banded and tridiagonal types. These cases raise a MethodError, without a hidden dense conversion.\nIndefinite PackedMatrix: no packed Bunch–Kaufman. factorize raises an ArgumentError.\nNo lu! on banded storage: it would need a buffer for the fill-in of pivoting.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tensors/#Tensors","page":"Tensors","text":"","title":"Tensors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tensors/#[einsum](@ref)-and-contractions","page":"Tensors","text":"einsum(spec, A...) evaluates an Einstein notation on dense arrays, SparseTensors or sparse matrices. Pairwise contractions go through the native GEMM (TTGT).\n\nusing OxiBLAS\nA = randn(16, 16, 16); B = randn(16, 16)\nC = einsum(\"abc,cd->abd\", A, B)            # allocating\nP = plan_einsum(\"abc,cd->abd\", A, B)       # reusable plan\nD = similar(C); ws = EinsumWorkspace(Float64)\neinsum!(D, P, A, B; workspace=ws)          # in place, reused workspace\nD ≈ C\ntrue\n\nPlans. plan_einsum(spec, A...; optimize) chooses the contraction order: :greedy by default, :beam, :optimal, or :none for the direct contraction. A plan is immutable and thread-safe.\nIn-place evaluation. einsum!(C, plan, A...; α, β, workspace) writes into C. Temporaries come from the EinsumWorkspace.\nSliced plans. plan_einsum_sliced bounds the memory by slicing the computation; einsum_layout_report describes the chosen memory layouts.\nExplicit contractions. contract(A, B, axesA, axesB) and contract! contract the given axes (explicit conjugations).\nSpecialized products. outer_product, mode_product(A, U, mode), batched_matmul and batched_matmul!.","title":"einsum and contractions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tensors/#Sparse-tensors","page":"Tensors","text":"SparseTensor(dims, coordinates, values) stores sorted linear indices (column-major order).\n\nX = SparseTensor((30, 30, 30), [(rand(1:30), rand(1:30), rand(1:30)) for _ in 1:500], randn(500))\nU = randn(30, 4)\nY = einsum(\"abc,cz->abz\", X, U)            # sparse × dense\nsize(Y)\n(30, 30, 4)\n\nSparse × sparse products use a column-wise SpGEMM on the unfoldings.\nMatrix operands (CSR, CSC, SparseMatrixCSC, 2-D SparseTensor) stay in native CSC.\neinsum! into a sparse destination receives the CSC result directly.\nThe result of a sparse computation is a SparseTensor.","title":"Sparse tensors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tensors/#Decompositions","page":"Tensors","text":"Function Result\nhosvd(A; ranks) Tucker (TuckerFactor)\ntensor_train(A; maxrank, rtol) tensor train (TensorTrainFactor)\ncp_als(A, rank) CP by alternating least squares (CPFactor)\nreconstruct(F) reconstructed tensor","title":"Decompositions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tensors/#Performance","page":"Tensors","text":"The high-level suite compares einsum with TensorOperations (Performance).","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"One page per source family (src/<family>/). Each page lists:\n\nthe docstrings of the exported names of the family;\nfor the high-level API, the non-exported functions (OxiBLAS.lu, OxiBLAS.ldiv…), called with the OxiBLAS. prefix;\nthe exported names that do not have a docstring yet: their convention is described in the guides.\n\nThese pages are generated by docs/make.jl from the docstrings and from the source file of each method.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#How-do-I…?","page":"How do I…?","text":"Task-oriented recipes. Each example runs when the documentation is built. Every function named here has its full description in the API reference (search box, top left).\n\nusing OxiBLAS, LinearAlgebra, SparseArrays\n\nThe sparse examples use a 2-D Laplacian, symmetric positive definite:\n\nk = 30\nT1 = spdiagm(-1 => -ones(k-1), 0 => 2ones(k), 1 => -ones(k-1))\nL = kron(sparse(1.0I, k, k), T1) + kron(T1, sparse(1.0I, k, k))   # 900×900\nA = CSRMatrix(L)                                                 # OxiBLAS format\nb = ones(size(A, 1))","title":"How do I…?"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Solve-a-linear-system","page":"How do I…?","text":"","title":"Solve a linear system"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Dense,-direct","page":"How do I…?","text":"M = randn(200, 200) + 200I; c = randn(200)\nx = OxiBLAS.ldiv(M, c)              # factorize + solve (LU here)\nF = OxiBLAS.lu(M)                   # keep the factorization to reuse it\nx2 = F \\ c                          # or OxiBLAS.ldiv(F, c)\ny = similar(c); OxiBLAS.ldiv!(y, F, c)   # in place, no allocation of the result\nnorm(M * y - c) / norm(c)\n6.91893767385556e-16\n\nThe engine-level form is lu_factor + solve; use cholesky_factor for Hermitian positive definite matrices and ldlt_factor / bunchkaufman_factor for symmetric indefinite ones. OxiBLAS.factorize(M) chooses from the structure.","title":"Dense, direct"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Sparse,-direct","page":"How do I…?","text":"x = A \\ b                           # multifrontal LU or Cholesky, chosen from the structure\nF = OxiBLAS.factorize(A)            # reuse: Cholesky here (symmetric positive definite)\nx2 = OxiBLAS.ldiv(F, b)\ny = similar(b); OxiBLAS.ldiv!(y, F, b)   # in-place solve (0 allocations for Cholesky)\nnorm(L * y - b) / norm(b)\n2.545157273779934e-14\n\nThe engines are multifrontal_lu, multifrontal_cholesky, sparse_ldlt (indefinite) and sparse_qr (least squares). For in-place solves (OxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, F, B) or LinearAlgebra.ldiv!(F, B)), Cholesky reuses its internal supernodal buffer under its lock (0 allocations) and LU / LDLt / BunchKaufman / QRSparse solve in place with a single column-sized working vector.","title":"Sparse, direct"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Iteratively-(Krylov-methods)","page":"How do I…?","text":"Every solver returns a named tuple (; x, converged, iterations, residual, reason); residual is the recomputed true residual norm. Common keywords: x0 (initial guess), rtol, atol, maxiter, preconditioner.\n\nr = cg(A, b; rtol=1e-10)                              # Hermitian positive definite\n(r.converged, r.iterations, r.residual)\n(true, 62, 1.1354914635744317e-9)\n\nr = pcg(A, b; preconditioner=ic0(A), rtol=1e-10)      # incomplete Cholesky\n(r.converged, r.iterations)\n(true, 32)\n\nN = CSRMatrix(L + 0.3 * spdiagm(1 => ones(size(L,1)-1)))  # nonsymmetric\nr = gmres(N, b; preconditioner=ilu0(N), restart=30, rtol=1e-10)\n(r.converged, r.iterations)\n(true, 18)\n\nSolver Matrix\ncg, pcg Hermitian positive definite\nminres Hermitian, possibly indefinite\ngmres, fgmres (flexible: variable preconditioner) general\nbicgstab, tfqmr, qmr, idrs general, short recurrences (less memory than GMRES)\nblock_cg, block_gmres several right-hand sides at once\n\nPreconditioners: ilu0, ilut, ic0, ict, amg (algebraic multigrid), spai, ainv, relaxation_preconditioner (Gauss–Seidel, SOR, SSOR), polynomial_preconditioner, additive_schwarz, a vector (Jacobi: the diagonal), or a sparse direct factor.\n\nr = pcg(A, b; preconditioner=amg(A), rtol=1e-10)      # multigrid: few iterations\n(r.converged, r.iterations)\n(true, 9)\n\nB = randn(size(A, 1), 4)                             # four right-hand sides\nr = block_cg(A, B; rtol=1e-8)\n(r.converged, r.iterations, maximum(r.residuals))\n(true, 66, 2.478583659529529e-7)","title":"Iteratively (Krylov methods)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Least-squares","page":"How do I…?","text":"Tall = randn(300, 40); d = randn(300)\nF_qr = OxiBLAS.qr(Tall)\nx = F_qr \\ d                                 # or OxiBLAS.ldiv(Tall, d)\ny = zeros(40); ws = zeros(300)               # 300-row workspace reused across solves\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(y, F_qr, d; workspace=ws)      # in-place least squares (0 allocations)\nnorm(Tall' * (Tall * y - d))                 # normal equations satisfied\n7.01619668321596e-14\n\nRank-deficient problems: qr_pivoted or complete_orthogonal (minimum-norm solution). Underdetermined (wide) minimum-norm problems: OxiBLAS.lq(Wide) or OxiBLAS.svd(Wide), where OxiBLAS.ldiv!(y, F, b) solves directly into y without needing a workspace. Sparse: OxiBLAS.qr(A_sparse) (or sparse_qr then solve).","title":"Least squares"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Check-a-solution","page":"How do I…?","text":"x = OxiBLAS.ldiv(M, c)\nbackward_error(M, x, c).error                # componentwise backward error\n1.0029884596549734e-15\n\nrefine_solve adds iterative refinement with condition estimates; expert_solve adds equilibration.","title":"Check a solution"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Compute-an-SVD","page":"How do I…?","text":"","title":"Compute an SVD"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Full-SVD","page":"How do I…?","text":"X = randn(120, 80)\nS = OxiBLAS.svd(X)                     # S.U, S.S, S.Vt (and S.V)\nnorm(S.U * Diagonal(S.S) * S.Vt - X) / norm(X)\n3.7624765612533834e-15\n\nF = svd_factor(X; algorithm=:golub_kahan)   # engine: :jacobi (default, most accurate),\nF.S[1:3]                                    # :golub_kahan, :divide_conquer (fastest)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 19.495200981032674\n 19.383590811083003\n 18.937290837490476","title":"Full SVD"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Singular-values-only","page":"How do I…?","text":"OxiBLAS.svdvals(X)[1:3]                # or singular_values(X)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 19.49520098103266\n 19.383590811083092\n 18.937290837490437","title":"Singular values only"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#A-few-singular-triplets","page":"How do I…?","text":"T5 = svd_truncated(X, 5)               # the 5 largest, without the full SVD\nR5 = svd_randomized(X, 5)              # randomized range finder: fastest for low ranks\nI3 = svd_selected(X; indices=1:3)      # chosen indices (or interval=(lo, hi))\n(T5.S, R5.S[1:2], I3.S)\n([19.495200981032664, 19.383590811083078, 18.93729083749045, 18.4596402186527, 17.647911688329764], [19.223205956682925, 19.14044918718891], [19.495200981032664, 19.383590811083078, 18.93729083749045])","title":"A few singular triplets"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Sparse-matrix","page":"How do I…?","text":"s = sparse_svd_lanczos(A, 4)           # Golub–Kahan–Lanczos with thick restart\n(s.converged, s.S)\n(true, [7.979477293567565, 7.948798529288784, 7.948798529288773, 7.918119765010022])\n\nsparse_svd is the randomized variant. For data that arrives by rows or columns, incremental_svd is updated by svd_add_rows! / svd_add_columns!.","title":"Sparse matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Compute-eigenvalues","page":"How do I…?","text":"","title":"Compute eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Dense","page":"How do I…?","text":"H = Symmetric(randn(100, 100))\nE = OxiBLAS.eigen(H)                   # Hermitian: real values, orthonormal vectors\nG = OxiBLAS.eigen(randn(100, 100))     # general: complex values\n(E.values[1:2], G.values[1:2])\n([-18.719418863145318, -17.801830760260412], ComplexF64[-9.376642490874861 + 0.0im, -9.185409310727097 - 4.008824082462127im])\n\nEngines: eigen_hermitian (with algorithm), eigen_hermitian_dc (divide and conquer), eigen_hermitian_mrrr (subset by indices or interval), eigen_general, schur_factor. Generalized problems: OxiBLAS.eigen(A, B), eigen_generalized (Hermitian definite), eigen_pencil (QZ).","title":"Dense"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#A-few-eigenvalues-of-a-large-(sparse)-matrix","page":"How do I…?","text":"r = lobpcg(A; nev=3, which=:smallest, preconditioner=ic0(A))   # Hermitian; preconditioned\n(r.converged, r.iterations, r.values)\n(true, 43, [0.020522706432419442, 0.05120147071122069, 0.05120147071122109])\n\nr = lanczos(A; nev=3, which=:largest, maxiter=300)   # Hermitian Lanczos; maxiter = subspace size\n(r.converged, r.values)\n(true, [7.979477293567608, 7.948798529288833, 7.948798529288766])\n\nr = shift_invert(A; sigma=1.0, nev=3)           # eigenvalues closest to sigma\nreal.(r.values)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.983012096841086\n 0.9805392794340742\n 1.0270948026155102\n\nCheck r.converged: the iteration budgets (maxiter, maxrestarts) are finite, and an unconverged result is returned, not raised. Non-Hermitian: arnoldi, iram (implicit restarts), thick_restart_arnoldi. All eigenvalues in an interval: eigenvalues_in_interval (Chebyshev filtering).","title":"A few eigenvalues of a large (sparse) matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Other-matrix-computations","page":"How do I…?","text":"P = Symmetric(randn(50, 50)) + 60I\n(OxiBLAS.exp(P) ≈ exp(Matrix(P)), OxiBLAS.sqrt(P)^2 ≈ P, matrix_power(P, 3) ≈ Matrix(P)^3)\n(true, true, true)\n\nD = randn(30, 10) * randn(10, 20)              # rank 10\n(numerical_rank(D), size(null_space(D)), condition_number(randn(5, 5)) > 1)\n(10, (20, 10), true)\n\nAlso: OxiBLAS.det, logabsdet, inv, pinv (pseudoinverse), OxiBLAS.log, sin, cos, sylvester, lyap, frechet_exp, kron_product, kronecker_action.","title":"Other matrix computations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Multiply-matrices","page":"How do I…?","text":"P1 = randn(300, 200); P2 = randn(200, 100); C = zeros(300, 100)\nw = GemmWorkspace(Float64, 300, 100, 200)      # reused buffers: no allocation per call\ngemm!(C, P1, P2, 1.0, 0.0; workspace=w)        # C ← 1·P1·P2 + 0·C\nOxiBLAS.mul!(C, P1, P2)                        # high-level: also structured and sparse operands\nC ≈ P1 * P2\ntrue\n\ny = zeros(size(A, 1)); OxiBLAS.mul!(y, A, b)   # sparse × dense (A*b)\nz = OxiBLAS.mul(A, A)                          # sparse × sparse: sparse result\n(y ≈ L * b, nnz(SparseMatrixCSC(z)))\n(true, 11104)","title":"Multiply matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Work-with-sparse-matrices","page":"How do I…?","text":"As = sparse_permute(A, sortperm(rand(size(A, 1))))   # the Laplacian, randomly renumbered\np = reverse_cuthill_mckee(As)                        # bandwidth-reducing ordering\n(bandwidth_profile(As).bandwidth, bandwidth_profile(sparse_permute(As, p)).bandwidth)\n(1755, 61)\n\npath = tempname() * \".mtx\"\nwrite_matrix_market(path, A; symmetry=:symmetric)\nA2 = read_matrix_market(path)                  # CSRMatrix by default (format=:csc, :coo, :dense)\nA2 ≈ A\ntrue\n\nFormats: CSRMatrix, CSCMatrix, COOMatrix, DIAMatrix, ELLMatrix, BSRMatrix, HYBMatrix, SELLMatrix; each converts from a SparseMatrixCSC (CSRMatrix(S)) and back (SparseMatrixCSC(A)). Orderings: amd_ordering, nested_dissection, select_sparse_ordering (compares several).","title":"Work with sparse matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/howto/#Work-with-tensors","page":"How do I…?","text":"X3 = randn(8, 9, 10); Y2 = randn(10, 7)\nZ = einsum(\"abc,cd->abd\", X3, Y2)              # Einstein summation\nPl = plan_einsum(\"abc,cd->abd\", X3, Y2)        # plan once, run many times\nZ2 = similar(Z); einsum!(Z2, Pl, X3, Y2)\n(size(Z), Z ≈ Z2, contract(X3, Y2, (3,), (1,)) ≈ Z)\n((8, 9, 7), true, true)\n\nTk = hosvd(randn(6, 7, 8); ranks=(3, 3, 3))     # Tucker decomposition\nsize(reconstruct(Tk))\n(6, 7, 8)\n\nAlso tensor_train, cp_als, sparse tensors (SparseTensor) and batched_matmul!.","title":"Work with tensors"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/spectral/#Spectral-and-iterative-algorithms","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Sources: src/spectral/.","title":"Spectral and iterative algorithms"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.BlockKrylovFactor","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Block Krylov projection, with variable-width blocks after rank deflation. Approximately Abasis = basisprojected + residualbasis*residualcoupling. images stores A*basis; the large operator is never converted to a dense matrix. For a Hermitian operator, the projection is block tridiagonal up to rounding before a thick restart. Full two-pass reorthogonalization is always used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BlockKrylovFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.ChebyshevFilter","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Owned Chebyshev window filter of a Hermitian matrix, with Jackson damping by default. The sparse matrix and its scaled copy are retained. Spectral bounds provided by the caller assert an enclosure; they are not independently certified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ChebyshevFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.FilterWorkspace","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Reusable four-buffer Chebyshev recurrence workspace. Sharing serializes calls; metadata and native GEMM/SpMM packing may still allocate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.FilterWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.IncrementalSVDFactor","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Mutable, rank-capped native SVD state for appending rows or columns. Updates factor the stored approximation augmented with the new block; discarded directions from earlier updates cannot be recovered. Use svdsnapshot for a coherent independent copy while other tasks may update this state. lasttruncationnorm and lastprojection_residual are local diagnostics, not a certified cumulative error bound for the original data stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.IncrementalSVDFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.SparseSVDWorkspace","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"SparseSVDWorkspace(T, m, n, rank; subspace, blocksize, rowblock=64)\nSparseSVDWorkspace(A, rank; kwargs...)\n\nBuffers of sparse_svd_lanczos for an m×n matrix and the given rank: pass it as workspace= to repeated calls to avoid reallocating the Krylov bases. Not shareable between concurrent calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseSVDWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.StochasticConfig","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Reproducible stochastic estimates with a private random stream per call. probe_type is :rademacher, :gaussian or :spherical (covariance I). confidence is metadata, not a certified confidence interval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.StochasticConfig"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.apply_filter!","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Apply the window polynomial to vectors or column blocks. The result is complete before aliased output is changed. alpha=0 avoids reading input values; beta=0 avoids reading previous output values. Plan/workspace storage aliases are rejected. Nonfinite input/recurrence values are rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.apply_filter!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.arnoldi","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Twice-reorthogonalized Arnoldi Ritz pairs for real/complex operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.arnoldi"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.block_arnoldi","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"General block Arnoldi Ritz pairs, with true operator residuals. No restart is implied; rank loss shrinks blocks rather than launching scalar solvers. A converged residual does not certify globally extremal selection. Convergence requires norm(Av-lambdav)<=max(atol,rtol*abs(lambda)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.block_arnoldi"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.block_arnoldi_factor","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Build a rank-deflating block Arnoldi projection; maxblocks counts blocks, subspace bounds stored basis columns. Initial columns may be dependent. The default factor reserves at most ten blocks, never n columns unconditionally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.block_arnoldi_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.block_lanczos","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Hermitian block Lanczos Ritz pairs; algebraic, magnitude, bothends or closest targets. Full reorthogonalization trades additional work/storage for robustness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.block_lanczos"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.block_lanczos_factor","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Fully reorthogonalized Hermitian block Lanczos projection. check_hermitian=false is a caller assertion, not an inferred property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.block_lanczos_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.count_eigenvalues_in_interval","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Count with provenance. Returns a NamedTuple, not an unqualified integer. Diagonal counts with boundary_tol=0 and exact endpoint conversion are exact comparisons. Tridiagonal counts use scaled Sturm recurrences in working precision (not certified intervals). General operators report only the verified pairs of a bounded projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.count_eigenvalues_in_interval"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.eigenvalues_in_interval","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Find verified Hermitian eigenpairs in the closed interval [low,high]. :auto handles diagonal/tridiagonal structure directly, otherwise builds one bounded block Krylov projection. General partial projections never assert a complete interval count. boundary_tol explicitly widens numerical selection. Current tridiagonal vector extraction computes the full native MRRR spectrum; even vectors=false still forms vectors internally to verify true residuals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigenvalues_in_interval"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.filter_response","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Evaluate the scalar polynomial response (no interval membership guarantee).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.filter_response"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.hermitian_spectral_bounds","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Gershgorin spectral enclosure estimate for a finite Hermitian matrix. Computed in working precision with a rounding margin, not directed-rounding interval arithmetic. An exactly diagonal matrix retains its exact extrema.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hermitian_spectral_bounds"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.incremental_svd","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Initialize a native incremental SVD. maxrank defaults to the requested rank, so increasing the retained rank later requires a larger explicit cap here. Dense input defaults to :jacobi and also accepts :randomized; sparse input defaults to :randomized (at most rank 16 unless requested) and accepts :lanczos. Only the factor bases are retained, not a copy of the original matrix. Zero requested rank creates a zero approximation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.incremental_svd"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.initialize_svd!","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Replace a state's initial matrix, preserving its cap/tolerances/options. On success resets the update count; on failure leaves the old state intact. Initialization keywords such as method, seed and sketch sizes are forwarded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.initialize_svd!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.iram","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Implicitly restarted Arnoldi. Unwanted Ritz shifts are chased through the small Hessenberg matrix; the transformed Arnoldi basis and residual are retained. No dense expansion of A and no external eigensolver are used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.iram"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.lobpcg","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Locally optimal block preconditioned conjugate-gradient eigenpairs. Supports a Hermitian positive-definite metric B for AX=BX*Diagonal(values). The Rayleigh–Ritz subspace is span(X, preconditioned residual, old direction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lobpcg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.polynomial_filter","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"polynomial_filter(A, low, high; degree=20, bounds=nothing, damping=:jackson) -> ChebyshevFilter\n\nChebyshev polynomial approximating the indicator of the interval [low, high] of the spectrum of a Hermitian A (Jackson damping by default). Apply it with apply_filter!; used by eigenvalues_in_interval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.polynomial_filter"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.polynomial_filtered_eigenvalues","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Filtered block Lanczos with original-operator Rayleigh–Ritz extraction. Retains multiplicities by vector subspaces, never by deduplicating equal values. Requested-pair convergence does not imply completeness of the interval. Constant filters fall back to ordinary block Lanczos. Restarts retain original Ritz vectors and residual enrichment; no partial reorthogonalization or hard locking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.polynomial_filtered_eigenvalues"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.reorthogonalize_svd!","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"reorthogonalize_svd!(F::IncrementalSVDFactor; threaded=true) -> F\n\nRestores the orthogonality of the singular vectors of an incremental SVD, lost to rounding after many svd_add_rows!/svd_add_columns! updates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.reorthogonalize_svd!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.shift_invert","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"shift_invert(A; sigma=0, B=nothing, kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nEigenvalues of A (or of the pencil (A, B)) nearest to sigma: implicitly restarted Arnoldi (iram) on (A - σB)⁻¹, applied through one sparse LU. Keywords go to iram (nev, subspace, maxrestarts…). Returns the eigenvalues of A, the vectors and their residuals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.shift_invert"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.sparse_svd_lanczos","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Sparse singular triplets with bounded two-sided Golub-Kahan bases. workspace reuses basis, projection, block scratch, Ritz and packing storage; results own their arrays. algorithm=:dilation explicitly retains the prior Hermitian-dilation solver. No automatic normal-matrix/full-spectrum fallback. Default results do not retain the Krylov basis; return_basis=true copies it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_svd_lanczos"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.sparse_svd_workspace_bytes","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Owned numeric payload bytes (including packing); excludes outputs, sparse input, locks/headers, transient small projected factorizations and task metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_svd_workspace_bytes"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.stochastic_diagonal","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Unbiased diagonal estimate; entrywise standard errors use complex |.|².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.stochastic_diagonal"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.stochastic_frobenius","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Rectangular Frobenius estimate. squaredestimate is unbiased; its square root estimate is generally biased. squaredstd_error applies to the square.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.stochastic_frobenius"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.stochastic_logdet","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Stochastic Lanczos quadrature logdet for Hermitian positive-definite A. Hermitian symmetry is checked exactly by default; checkhermitian=false is a caller assertion. Positive Ritz values do not certify global definiteness. stderror excludes Lanczos truncation bias; no quadrature error bound is claimed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.stochastic_logdet"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.svd_add_columns!","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Append an m-by-q column block, using a rank-revealing residual basis and a native coupling SVD. A zero residual still changes singular values/vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_add_columns!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.svd_add_rows!","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Append a p-by-n block of rows, always refactoring the coupling SVD even if the residual block has rank zero. The input is copied under the state lock, so views of owned factor data are accepted without in-place corruption.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_add_rows!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.svd_rank","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Current retained rank, read under the state guard without copying factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_rank"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.svd_snapshot","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"svd_snapshot(F::IncrementalSVDFactor) -> NamedTuple\n\nCopy of the current state of an incremental SVD: (; U, S, Vt, shape, maxrank, updates, ...), independent of later updates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.thick_restart_arnoldi","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Thick-restarted block Arnoldi / Krylov-Schur for general dense or sparse A. Retains whole real Schur blocks and cached operator images. locking=true soft-locks wanted converged Ritz pairs by retaining their Schur subspace; it never projects with nonorthogonal individual eigenvectors. :selective uses norm-loss/orthogonality checks to trigger a second projection; default :full always uses two passes. True operator residuals decide convergence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.thick_restart_arnoldi"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.thick_restart_lanczos","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Thick-restarted Hermitian block Krylov eigensolver. Retains keep Ritz vectors together with boundary residual directions, then expands the retained subspace with full reorthogonalization. Uses a full small Rayleigh–Ritz projection, not a short-recurrence tridiagonal after restarting. No hard locking, interval filtering or generalized metric is implied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.thick_restart_lanczos"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.trace_hutch_plus_plus","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Hutch++ with independent range sketch and residual probes. num_probes budgets operator applications (unused rank-deficient sketch columns can reduce that count). The returned SE is conditional sampling error only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trace_hutch_plus_plus"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.trace_hutchinson","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Hutchinson trace estimate for real/complex square operators. Complex probes use four unit phases; std_error measures E|error|², not separate real/imaginary confidence intervals. A single probe has unknown (Inf) error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trace_hutchinson"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.trace_xtrace","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Optimized exchangeable XTrace, dense or native sparse, real or complex. num_probes retains its existing meaning: p independent input probes, requiring p+rank(A*Omega) <= 2p operator products. normalization=:auto respheres spherical probes; :resphere also accepts Gaussian probes, and :none matches the explicit leave-one-out estimator. Rademacher resphering is rejected because it is biased. The empirical exchangeable error estimate is heuristic; std_error remains NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trace_xtrace"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/spectral/#OxiBLAS.trace_xtrace_loo","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Exchangeable leave-one-out trace control variate (XTrace family). Each probe is independent of the range basis formed from all other probes. This implementation rebuilds those bases; it does not implement the optimized inverse-triangular XTrace formula. Correlated samples: stderror is NaN. numprobes counts input probes, not the more numerous operator applications.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trace_xtrace_loo"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/blas/#BLAS-1,-2,-3-and-matrix-multiplication","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and matrix multiplication","text":"Every function works on 1-based arrays. The optimized paths cover Float32, Float64, ComplexF32 and ComplexF64. Outputs must be mutable and free of repeated indices.","title":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and matrix multiplication"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/blas/#GEMM","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and matrix multiplication","text":"using OxiBLAS\nA = randn(Float32, 300, 200); B = randn(Float32, 200, 150); C = zeros(Float32, 300, 150)\ngemm!(C, A, B)                           # C ← A·B\ngemm!(C, A, B, 2f0, 1f0; transA=:N)      # C ← 2·A·B + C\nG = zeros(Float32, 200, 200)\ngemm!(G, A, A; transA=:C)                # G ← Aᴴ·A\nkernel_info(Float32)\n(architecture = :x86_64, cpu = \"znver4\", kind = :scalar, lanes = 1, mr = 4, nr = 4)\n\ntransA/transB are :N, :T or :C. Non-unit-stride views, transpose and adjoint are accepted.\nAn input that overlaps the output is copied before writing. β = 0 does not read the old output; α = 0 or k = 0 does not read the inputs.\nalgorithm selects a path: :auto, :reference, :blocked, :packed, :register, :simd. kernel is :auto, :scalar, :neon, :avx2 or :avx512; an unavailable ISA raises an error before anything is written.\nWorkspace. GemmWorkspace(T, m, n, k; config, workers) reserves reusable panels. Sharing it between calls is protected by a lock; for independent concurrent calls, use one workspace per task.\nParallelism. It is controlled by julia --threads=N, threaded=false and GemmConfig(parallel_threshold=...). The number of tasks follows the size of the product.\nVariants: gemm_cache_oblivious!, gemm_strassen!, gemm_winograd! (explicitly chosen), gemm_batched!, gemm_strided_batched!, gemm_extended! (generic types).","title":"GEMM"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/blas/#OxiBLAS.mul!-and-OxiBLAS.mul","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and matrix multiplication","text":"OxiBLAS.mul!(C, A, B, α, β) and OxiBLAS.mul(A, B) (not exported) dispatch to the right kernel from the types: dense GEMM, sparse × dense, sparse × sparse, structured matrices. A * B on the OxiBLAS sparse formats goes through the same layer.","title":"OxiBLAS.mul! and OxiBLAS.mul"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/blas/#BLAS-1","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and matrix multiplication","text":"Function Convention\naxpy!(y, x, α=1), scal!(x, α) y ← y + αx ; x ← αx\ncopy_vector!(y, x), swap!(x, y) copy with snapshot; swap of disjoint vectors\ndotu(x, y), dotc(x, y) sum without / with conjugation of the first operand\ndot_kahan, dot_pairwise compensated and pairwise summation\ndsdot, sdsdot Float32 vectors, Float64 accumulation\nnrm2, asum, iamax, iamin scaled Euclidean norm; complex metric |Re|+|Im|\nrotg, rot!, rotmg, rotmg!, rotm!, RotmParams Givens and modified rotations\naxpy_parallel!, scal_parallel!, dot_parallel, nrm2_parallel, asum_parallel explicit multithreaded variants","title":"BLAS 1"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/blas/#BLAS-2","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and matrix multiplication","text":"Function Convention\ngemv!(y, A, x, α=1, β=0; trans=:N) y ← α·op(A)·x + β·y\nger!(A, x, y, α), gerc! rank-1 update, yᵀ / yᴴ\nsymv!, hemv! same order as gemv!, uplo=:U or :L\nsyr!, syr2!, her!, her2! only the selected triangle is modified\ntrmv!(x, A; uplo, trans, unit), trsv! a unit diagonal is never read","title":"BLAS 2"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/blas/#BLAS-3","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and matrix multiplication","text":"Function Convention\nsymm!(C, A, B, α, β; side, uplo), hemm! A is the structured matrix, side :L/:R\ntrmm!(B, A, α; ...), trsm! B is overwritten; side, uplo, trans, unit\nsyrk!(C, A, α, β; ...), herk!, syr2k!, her2k! only the triangle of C is updated\ngemmt!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; uplo, transA, transB) triangle of α·op(A)·op(B) + β·C\ntriangular_workspace reusable workspace for TRMM/TRSM\n\nThe structured BLAS 3 routines reuse the GEMM engine and its options (algorithm, kernel, threaded, workspace, config). SYRK, HERK, SYR2K, HER2K and GEMMT can spread independent tiles across tasks.\n\nMeasurements and methodology: Performance.","title":"BLAS 3"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/graphs/#Graphs-and-orderings","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Sources: src/graphs/.","title":"Graphs and orderings"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.bipartite_coloring","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Two-color the undirected off-diagonal pattern (diagonal entries ignored).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bipartite_coloring"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.bipartite_graph_matching","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Maximum-cardinality matching of a square undirected bipartite graph. The off-diagonal pattern is symmetrized; diagonal and numerical zeros ignored. Returns nothing for a non-bipartite graph. This differs from bipartite_matching, which treats a rectangular matrix as a row/column bipartite incidence pattern.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bipartite_graph_matching"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.column_minimum_degree","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Exact column minimum-degree on implicit row-clique elements. Ports the ordering semantics of Rust colamd/colamdaggressive, whose upstream documentation explicitly disclaims real COLAMD. No numerical A'*A or initial column-intersection graph is materialized. Overlapping elements are unioned to compute exact degrees; worst-case work/fill is still superlinear. densethreshold in [0,1] ignores rows denser than that fraction of columns. absorb removes elements contained in the new fill element; no supercolumn merging or SuiteSparse aggressive-absorption equivalence is implied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.column_minimum_degree"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.compare_orderings","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"compare_orderings(A; algorithms=(:natural, :rcm, :minimum_degree, :multiple_minimum_degree, :multilevel_nested_dissection))\n\nComputes each fill-reducing ordering and its metrics on the symmetric pattern of A: one named tuple per algorithm with permutation, factor_nnz (Cholesky fill), fill, fill_ratio, bandwidth and profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.compare_orderings"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.degree_sequence","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Off-diagonal undirected degrees; diagonal entries and numerical zeros ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.degree_sequence"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.graph_bandwidth_profile","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Graph convention used by upstream graph::bandwidthprofile: bandwidth is max(abs(i-j)), profile is the sum of each row's max-column minus min-column, including the diagonal location in the envelope, and averagebandwidth is profile/n. Distinct from bandwidth_profile's full matrix width/lower envelope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.graph_bandwidth_profile"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.is_structurally_symmetric","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"is_structurally_symmetric(A) -> Bool\n\nWhether the pattern of the off-diagonal nonzero entries of A is symmetric (values are not compared).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.is_structurally_symmetric"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.multilevel_nested_dissection","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Nested dissection using multilevel bisection and a greedy vertex cover of cross edges. Every reported separator removes all left/right cross edges. Leaf minimum-degree ordering is exact; separators need not be minimum size. return_tree=true exposes the separator tree for structural inspection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.multilevel_nested_dissection"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.multilevel_partition","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Native recursive k-way multilevel partitioning. Heavy-edge contraction, BFS initial bisection, greedy moves/pair swaps or locked best-prefix FM (refinement=:fm) on uncoarsening. Default :greedy preserves the prior policy. No METIS-equivalent quality or balance guarantee is claimed: balanced reports the achieved global load criterion. The algorithms are currently sequential.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.multilevel_partition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.multiple_minimum_degree","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Multiple minimum degree (Liu): every independent variable of minimum degree is eliminated in one round before the degrees are updated, on the quotient graph with supervariables of minimum_degree_ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.multiple_minimum_degree"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.ordering_metrics","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Exact symbolic fill count and bandwidth/profile for an explicit permutation. This can be expensive: symbolic fill is computed, not cheaply predicted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ordering_metrics"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.pseudo_peripheral_vertex","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Pseudo-peripheral vertex in the starting connected component. Empty graphs return 0; the iteration alternates minimum-degree vertices in the farthest BFS level until its depth stops increasing. No global-diameter guarantee.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.pseudo_peripheral_vertex"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.refine_partition","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Refine a supplied two-way labeling using locked best-prefix FM passes or existing greedy moves/pair swaps. Labels are copied, never modified in place. The retained result improves balance violation first, then cut, in working precision; balanced reports the requested left fraction, not an optimum. Temporary exploration can exceed the requested weight tolerance by one vertex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.refine_partition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.select_sparse_ordering","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"select_sparse_ordering(A; algorithms) -> (; best, comparisons)\n\nThe ordering of compare_orderings with the smallest Cholesky factor (ties broken by bandwidth then profile), with all the comparisons.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.select_sparse_ordering"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.sparse_ordering","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Select a native ordering; permutation[newindex] is the old index. :amd is approximate minimum degree on A+Aᵀ (Cholesky, LDLᵀ); `:amdataon AᵀA without dense rows (LU) and:amdqron AᵀA (QR), the COLAMD substitutes of CSparse.:minimumdegree` is the exact (slower) variant. Rectangular matrices support :natural, :columnminimumdegree, :amdata and :amdqr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_ordering"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.weighted_bipartite_matching","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Weighted row/column bipartite matching on the nonzero pattern of A. objective=:cardinality first maximizes cardinality, then total weight; :weight permits unmatched vertices to maximize weight (zero-cost ties may increase cardinality). weight=:auto uses signed real values or complex abs; :value, :abs and :logabs are also supported. Missing/zero entries are not edges. Uses scaled Float64 costs, sparse residual arcs and Dijkstra potentials. Optimality is in working precision, not an exact-arithmetic certificate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.weighted_bipartite_matching"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/graphs/#OxiBLAS.weighted_graph_matching","page":"Graphs and orderings","text":"Weighted matching of a square bipartite adjacency graph. Returns nothing for a non-bipartite pattern. Undirected weights use max of the two transformed directions, explicitly handling one-triangle/asymmetric storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.weighted_graph_matching"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/utils/#Utilities,-hardware-and-precision","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Sources: src/utils/.","title":"Utilities, hardware and precision"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.GemmTuning","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"GemmTuning\n\nResult of autotune_gemm: the fastest validated config (GemmConfig), the kernel, the timings of all candidates and the machine (cpu) they were measured on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.GemmTuning"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.autotune_gemm","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Explicit, opt-in native GEMM calibration. Calling this function runs timings; loading the package never calibrates or benchmarks. Candidate results are checked against the native reference before their measured time is accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.autotune_gemm"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.dot_extended","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Compensated dot product in a caller-selectable accumulation type. Float16/32 default to Float32/64; other providers preserve their element type. accumulator_type can explicitly select BigFloat, Double64, Float128, etc. Products are rounded by that type; no hidden fixed extended scalar is used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.dot_extended"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.gemm_extended!","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Compatibility entry point for GEMM with caller-provided floating types. Native Float32/64 retain specialized dispatch; other concrete AbstractFloat and complex floating types use generic Julia arithmetic. No scalar is wrapped or reimplemented by OxiBLAS. Float16 GEMM accumulates in Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_extended!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.generic_threadsafe","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Whether scalar arithmetic has a context safe to use in spawned kernel tasks. Machine floats are enabled. Other providers conservatively execute kernels in one task, preserving the caller's arithmetic context (notably BigFloat precision). Providers may extend this OxiBLAS function explicitly; no Base method is needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.generic_threadsafe"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.kernel_info","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Report selected kernel, vector width, and micro-panel dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.kernel_info"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.load_gemm_tuning","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Load tuning only for the same CPU, Julia version, thread count and type. Returns the config/kernel/shape; it never starts a new calibration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.load_gemm_tuning"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/utils/#OxiBLAS.save_gemm_tuning","page":"Utilities, hardware and precision","text":"Save an explicitly measured configuration in a simple versioned text file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.save_gemm_tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/tensors/#Tensors","page":"Tensors","text":"Sources: src/tensors/.","title":"Tensors"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS-methods-of-other-modules'-functions","page":"Tensors","text":"These functions belong to LinearAlgebra, SparseArrays or Base; OxiBLAS adds methods for its own types to them (see the guides):\n\nBase.setindex!, SparseArrays.findnz","title":"OxiBLAS methods of other modules' functions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.CPFactor","page":"Tensors","text":"CPFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of cp_als: CP (CANDECOMP/PARAFAC) decomposition with weights, one factor matrix per mode, iterations, relative_error, converged and reason; reconstruct rebuilds the tensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.CPFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.EinsumPlan","page":"Tensors","text":"EinsumPlan\n\nA contraction plan from plan_einsum: the parsed specification, the pairwise contraction order and its memory estimate. Reuse it with einsum! to contract arrays of the same shapes without planning again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.EinsumPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.EinsumWorkspace","page":"Tensors","text":"Reusable tensor-intermediate buffers, protected for concurrent callers. An execution leases separate buffers for simultaneously live intermediates. Returned arrays never expose these buffers; einsum! also reuses its result scratch. Packing/index metadata may still allocate. Retained capacity is not bounded by a plan's live-payload memory_limit. Use empty! between campaigns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.EinsumWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.SlicedEinsumPlan","page":"Tensors","text":"A contraction plan executed as serial tiles; arrays remain ordinary Julia arrays. scratch_limit bounds the modeled live tile/intermediate payload, excluding the caller-owned full output, inputs, alias snapshots, pool retention and packing. Tile kernels may use native threading; reductions across tiles are ordered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SlicedEinsumPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.SparseTensor","page":"Tensors","text":"SparseTensor{T,N} <: SparseArrays.AbstractSparseArray{T,Int,N}\n\nCoordinate sparse tensor of any order: stored entries are kept as sorted, unique column-major linear indices with their values. Explicit stored zeros are preserved; reshape only changes the dimensions (the linear indices are the same). Construct from coordinates (duplicates combined with combine), from a dense array (droptol), or from SparseMatrixCSC, SparseVector and the OxiBLAS sparse formats without any dense intermediate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseTensor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.TensorTrainFactor","page":"Tensors","text":"TensorTrainFactor{T}\n\nResult of tensor_train: the 3-D cores and the original shape; reconstruct rebuilds the tensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.TensorTrainFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.TuckerFactor","page":"Tensors","text":"TuckerFactor{T}\n\nResult of hosvd: core tensor and one factor matrix per mode; reconstruct rebuilds the tensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.TuckerFactor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.batched_matmul","page":"Tensors","text":"N-D batched matrix multiplication: dimensions 1/2 are matrix dimensions; remaining dimensions broadcast independently (including singleton batch axes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.batched_matmul"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.batched_matmul!","page":"Tensors","text":"batched_matmul!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; transA=:N, transB=:N) -> C\n\nBatched matrix product over the trailing dimensions: C[:,:,k...] ← α·op(A[:,:,k...])·op(B[:,:,k...]) + β·C[:,:,k...], with broadcasting of batch dimensions of size 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.batched_matmul!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.contract","page":"Tensors","text":"contract(A, B, axesA, axesB; conjugateA=false, conjugateB=false, output=:auto) -> Array\n\nTensor contraction over the axis pairs (axesA[i], axesB[i]) (one GEMM after a permutation). The result has the free axes of A then those of B. Sparse operands (or output=:sparse) give a sparse result.\n\nC = contract(A, B, (3,), (1,))     # Σ_k A[i,j,k]·B[k,l]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.contract"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.contract!","page":"Tensors","text":"contract!(C, A, B, axesA, axesB; α=1, β=0) -> C\n\nIn-place contract: C ← α·contract(A, B, axesA, axesB) + β·C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.contract!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.cp_als","page":"Tensors","text":"CANDECOMP/PARAFAC by alternating least squares with native MTTKRP/GEMM and LU solves. Real and complex arrays are supported. initial can provide factors; otherwise deterministic, nonrandom factors are used. Convergence and stagnation are reported separately from the attained approximation error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.cp_als"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.einsum","page":"Tensors","text":"Einstein summation on Julia arrays. Complex values are multiplied without implicit conjugation. Scalar expressions return a zero-dimensional Array. Operands may be sparse (SparseTensor, SparseMatrixCSC, SparseVector, OxiBLAS sparse formats, and their transposes/adjoints): they are contracted without densification. output=:auto returns a SparseTensor when every operand is sparse and a dense Array otherwise; :dense / :sparse force the kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.einsum"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.einsum!","page":"Tensors","text":"einsum!(C, plan, arrays...; α=1, β=0, workspace=nothing) -> C\n\nIn-place contraction C ← α·einsum(arrays...) + β·C with a precomputed EinsumPlan (or a sliced plan from plan_einsum_sliced). A reusable EinsumWorkspace avoids allocating the intermediates.\n\nP = plan_einsum(\"abc,cd->abd\", X, Y); ws = EinsumWorkspace(Float64)\neinsum!(Z, P, X, Y; workspace=ws)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.einsum!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.einsum_layout_report","page":"Tensors","text":"Explain a plan's arithmetic and physical-layout model without reading data. Traffic is a heuristic scalar-entry score, not measured bytes/time or a guarantee that a reshape copies. Reusing a plan on other strides keeps correctness but may change the merits of its path. No measured-speed optimality is claimed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.einsum_layout_report"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.hosvd","page":"Tensors","text":"Truncated higher-order SVD, using the native one-sided Jacobi SVD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hosvd"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.mode_product","page":"Tensors","text":"Multiply mode mode by U (new dimension × old dimension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.mode_product"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.outer_product","page":"Tensors","text":"outer_product(A, B) -> Array\n\nTensor (outer) product: C[i..., j...] = A[i...]·B[j...].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.outer_product"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.plan_einsum","page":"Tensors","text":"Build a reusable shape-checked contraction plan. Supports ellipsis broadcasting, repeated labels (diagonals/traces), scalar outputs, reductions, and any operand count. Greedy planning minimizes local pair work, then intermediate size. :beam keeps bounded-width candidate paths; :optimal exhaustively minimizes modeled pair work with peak payload as a tie-breaker. Both enforce search_limit and throw on exhaustion; optimality concerns this cost model, not measured runtime. :none bypasses pair planning and evaluates the all-operand contraction directly. memory_limit bounds live intermediate tensor payload in bytes, including the result and a conservative output permutation copy, excluding caller inputs, planner metadata, per-worker index vectors, and BLAS packing workspaces. It is not a process RSS cap. If no pair fits, contract all operands directly with no pair intermediates; this can be substantially slower. The output must fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.plan_einsum"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.plan_einsum_sliced","page":"Tensors","text":"Plan explicit named-label tiles, e.g. chunks=(i=16,k=32). A sliced reduction accumulates partial sums; slicing output labels writes disjoint output tiles. ASCII labels, repeated labels and unsliced broadcast ellipses are supported. There is no dense Tensor container and no process/RSS or out-of-core guarantee.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.plan_einsum_sliced"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.reconstruct","page":"Tensors","text":"reconstruct(F::Union{TuckerFactor, TensorTrainFactor, CPFactor}) -> Array\n\nDense tensor represented by a Tucker, tensor-train or CP decomposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.reconstruct"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/tensors/#OxiBLAS.tensor_train","page":"Tensors","text":"Sequential TT-SVD. maxrank caps each bond; rtol bounds the discarded Frobenius norm per split by rtol*norm(A)/sqrt(N-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tensor_train"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#Performance-and-benchmarks","page":"Performance and benchmarks","text":"","title":"Performance and benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#High-level-suite","page":"Performance and benchmarks","text":"benchmark/run.jl measures a generated grid: each high-level operation, on each input type it accepts, for Float16, Float32, Float64, ComplexF32, ComplexF64 and BigFloat, at three sizes (about 4,000 cases). The inputs are the matrix types of LinearAlgebra (Matrix, Hermitian, triangular, Diagonal, Bidiagonal, Tridiagonal, SymTridiagonal, SparseMatrixCSC) and those of OxiBLAS (CSRMatrix, CSCMatrix, COOMatrix, BandedMatrix, PackedMatrix). Each case is compared with the matching reference:\n\nFamily Reference\nproducts, BLAS 3 LinearAlgebra (OpenBLAS, Accelerate on macOS; generic code for Float16/BigFloat)\ndense factorizations, spectra, matrix functions LinearAlgebra (LAPACK, or its generic code)\n\\, OxiBLAS.ldiv, ldiv! the same problem with LinearAlgebra or SparseArrays\nsparse factorizations SparseArrays (UMFPACK, CHOLMOD, SPQR)\nBandedMatrix, PackedMatrix the same matrix as SparseMatrixCSC or Hermitian{Matrix}\neinsum TensorOperations\n\nWhen LinearAlgebra has no method (for example svd of a BigFloat matrix, or UMFPACK for Float32), only OxiBLAS is measured.\n\njulia --project=benchmark -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=benchmark --threads=1 benchmark/run.jl                     # whole grid\njulia --project=benchmark --threads=1 benchmark/run.jl lu 'ldiv!/*/f64/*'  # name prefixes and globs\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/run.jl --types=f32,f64 --sizes=s --families=solves\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/run.jl --allocs                        # allocation campaign only\nOXIBLAS_REFERENCE=accelerate julia --project=benchmark benchmark/run.jl\n\nMethod.\n\nSame conditions. Same mathematical problem, same data and same number of threads for OxiBLAS and the reference.\nVerification. Each OxiBLAS result is checked by a backward error or a reconstruction, which does not need the reference.\nWarm timings. A first call compiles and warms up; the reported times are medians of warm samples (Chairmarks).\nAllocations. Allocation count and bytes of one warm call, on both sides. The report lists the in-place cases that allocate and the cases that allocate more than the reference.\nSeparate forms. Allocating forms (OxiBLAS.lu(A), A \\ b), in-place forms (ldiv!, mul!, lu!) and sparse refactorizations (lu!(F, A), cholesky!(F, A)) are distinct cases.\nTargeted compilation. Only the selected cases are compiled.\nOutputs. Status, median times, ratio, allocation counts and bytes, in CSV and Markdown under benchmark/results/, with summaries by family, operation, element type and input.\n\nThe benchmarks are not run by the CI: timings on GitHub runners are not stable enough.","title":"High-level suite"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#Historical-campaigns","page":"Performance and benchmarks","text":"The kernel micro-benchmarks and full campaigns (BLAS 1/2/3, LAPACK, dense SVD, sparse, tensors, graphs) are archived in benchmark/archive/. They remain usable for future optimization work; their campaign reports are not part of the repository.","title":"Historical campaigns"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#Latency-and-precompilation","page":"Performance and benchmarks","text":"The package precompiles, with PrecompileTools, a small workload of common paths:\n\nFloat32 and Float64 GEMM;\ndense LU and Cholesky;\na sparse factorization and its solve;\nOxiBLAS.ldiv and \\ on a CSRMatrix;\nan einsum contraction.\n\nThe first call of these paths therefore no longer pays the compilation. Other types and variants compile on first use.\n\nMeasurements on an Apple M5 Max, Julia 1.14, each path in a fresh process (benchmark/latency/run_all.jl, results in benchmark/latency/latency_*.tsv):\n\nFirst call Without workload With workload\ngemm! Float64 0.38 s 0.09 s\nOxiBLAS.lu(A) \\ b 2.85 s 0.10 s\nOxiBLAS.cholesky 2.74 s 0.01 s\nOxiBLAS.ldiv(A, b) 13.5 s 0.13 s\nsparse A \\ b (Cholesky, LU) 7.2 s 0.005 s\neinsum 3.2 s 0.06 s\nusing OxiBLAS 0.03 s 0.15 s\n\nCost of the workload. Package precompilation goes from about 3 s to 20 s, and its cache from 10 MB to about 50 MB.\n\nDisabling the workload. Add precompile_workload = false to the [OxiBLAS] section of LocalPreferences.toml.","title":"Latency and precompilation"},{"category":"section","location":"#OxiBLAS.jl","page":"Home","text":"OxiBLAS.jl is a linear algebra library written entirely in Julia. It is an adapted and extended port of the Rust library OxiBLAS (reference pinned at commit 8d82bb6).\n\nNative computation. No computation is delegated to BLAS, LAPACK, Accelerate, SuiteSparse or the original Rust code. Those libraries are only references in the tests and benchmarks. LinearAlgebra provides the package with its wrapper types (Transpose, Adjoint, Hermitian…), its exceptions and the Factorization interface, never its computations.\nSIMD micro-kernels. They use LLVM vectors (llvmcall), without SIMD.jl or LoopVectorization.jl. NEON is validated on Apple Silicon; the AVX2 and AVX-512 variants are implemented but not validated on x86 hardware.\nTypes. The optimized paths cover Float32, Float64, ComplexF32 and ComplexF64. Many algorithms also accept generic floating-point types (BigFloat, Float16, extended-precision types), without the same performance.","title":"OxiBLAS.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"The package is not registered yet:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/yolhan83/OxiBLAS.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#First-examples","page":"Home","text":"Native matrix multiplication. gemm! writes C ← α·op(A)·op(B) + β·C, with an optional reusable workspace:\n\nusing OxiBLAS\nA = randn(513, 257); B = randn(257, 389); C = zeros(513, 389)\nw = GemmWorkspace(Float64, 513, 389, 257)\ngemm!(C, A, B, 0.7, 0.0; workspace=w)\nC ≈ 0.7 * A * B\ntrue\n\nHigh-level API. It reuses the names of LinearAlgebra, but its functions belong to OxiBLAS and are not exported:\n\nusing LinearAlgebra      # only for I and the check\nM = randn(50, 50) + 50I\nF = OxiBLAS.lu(M)        # native LU (a LinearAlgebra.Factorization)\nb = randn(50)\nx = OxiBLAS.ldiv(F, b)   # reuses F; F \\ b gives the same result\nM * x ≈ b\ntrue\n\nSparse matrices. The OxiBLAS formats (CSRMatrix, CSCMatrix, COOMatrix…) support \\, with a factorization chosen from the structure, as in SparseArrays:\n\nusing SparseArrays\nS = sprand(200, 200, 0.02) + 10I\nA = CSRMatrix(S)\nx = A \\ ones(200)\nS * x ≈ ones(200)\ntrue\n\nTensors. einsum contracts dense or sparse arrays:\n\nX = randn(8, 9, 10); Y = randn(10, 7)\nZ = einsum(\"abc,cd->abd\", X, Y)\nsize(Z)\n(8, 9, 7)","title":"First examples"},{"category":"section","location":"#Organization-of-the-documentation","page":"Home","text":"How do I…? gives task-oriented recipes: solving a system (direct or iterative), least squares, SVD, eigenvalues, products, sparse matrices, tensors.\nThe guides present each family of features and its conventions.\nThe API reference groups the docstrings by source family.","title":"Organization of the documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Names-shared-with-LinearAlgebra","page":"Home","text":"OxiBLAS.lu, OxiBLAS.qr, OxiBLAS.svd, OxiBLAS.ldiv, OxiBLAS.exp, etc. are functions distinct from those of LinearAlgebra and Base (OxiBLAS.lu !== LinearAlgebra.lu). They are not exported, so using LinearAlgebra, OxiBLAS creates no name conflict. Extensions of LinearAlgebra and Base functions (for instance A \\ b on a CSRMatrix) only concern signatures that contain an OxiBLAS type: no method is added for Matrix or SparseMatrixCSC.","title":"Names shared with LinearAlgebra"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/highlevel/#High-level-API","page":"High-level API","text":"The high-level API reuses the names and conventions of LinearAlgebra, on top of the native engines.\n\nFunctions of OxiBLAS. OxiBLAS.lu !== LinearAlgebra.lu. They are not exported: write OxiBLAS.lu(A), OxiBLAS.ldiv(F, b).\nLinearAlgebra types. The factorizations (OxiBLAS.LU, OxiBLAS.Cholesky, OxiBLAS.QR…) are subtypes of LinearAlgebra.Factorization: F \\ b, ldiv!(Y, F, b), F', inv(F), det(F) work as for the factorizations of LinearAlgebra.\nNo hidden fallback. On a matrix that no OxiBLAS engine supports, the call raises a MethodError, never a silent BLAS/LAPACK computation.\n\nusing OxiBLAS, LinearAlgebra\nA = randn(60, 60) + 60I; B = randn(60, 3)\nF = OxiBLAS.lu(A)\nX = F \\ B                       # generic LinearAlgebra code, OxiBLAS primitives\nF2 = OxiBLAS.factorize(Hermitian(A * A' + I))   # Cholesky, chosen from the structure\nS = OxiBLAS.svd(randn(40, 20))\n(typeof(F2), size(S.U), norm(A * X - B) < 1e-10)\n(OxiBLAS.Cholesky{Float64, CholeskyFactor{Float64, Matrix{Float64}}}, (40, 20), true)","title":"High-level API"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/highlevel/#Factorizations-and-functions","page":"High-level API","text":"Function Engine\nOxiBLAS.lu, lu! lu_factor (dense), multifrontal_lu (sparse)\nOxiBLAS.cholesky, cholesky! cholesky_factor, multifrontal_cholesky\nOxiBLAS.ldlt, bunchkaufman (and !) ldlt_factor, sparse_ldlt\nOxiBLAS.qr (NoPivot, ColumnNorm, sparse), qr!, lq, lq! qr_factor, qr_pivoted, sparse_qr\nOxiBLAS.svd, svdvals (and !) svd_factor, singular_values\nOxiBLAS.eigen, eigvals, eigvecs, eigmax, eigmin eigen_hermitian, eigen_general, eigenvalues\nOxiBLAS.eigen(A, B), eigvals(A, B) eigen_generalized or QZ (eigen_pencil)\nOxiBLAS.schur(A), schur(A, B), ordschur, hessenberg schur_factor, qz_factor, hessenberg_factor\nOxiBLAS.factorize choice from the structure (see below)\nOxiBLAS.det, logdet, logabsdet, inv, rank, cond, pinv, nullspace, isposdef through the engines; inv of a sparse matrix is refused\nOxiBLAS.norm, opnorm, kron, sylvester, lyap native engines\nOxiBLAS.exp, log, sqrt, sin, cos matrix functions; on a number, those of Base\nOxiBLAS.mul, mul! dense, sparse and structured products\nOxiBLAS.spdiagm, sparsevec sparse constructors in the OxiBLAS formats","title":"Factorizations and functions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/highlevel/#Linear-solves:-ldiv,-ldiv!-and-\\","page":"High-level API","text":"Form Semantics\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, F, B; workspace=nothing) reuses the factorization F; Y receives the solution, B is untouched\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, A, B; workspace=nothing) first factorizes with OxiBLAS.factorize(A)\nOxiBLAS.ldiv(F, B), OxiBLAS.ldiv(A, B) allocating forms, Vector or Matrix like B\nA \\ B, LinearAlgebra.ldiv!(Y, A, B) on the OxiBLAS sparse formats and their wrappers\nLinearAlgebra.ldiv!(A, B) square systems: B is overwritten with the solution\n\nusing SparseArrays\nS = sprand(300, 300, 0.01) + 10I; A = CSRMatrix(S); b = randn(300)\nF = OxiBLAS.factorize(A)\nx1 = A \\ b; x2 = OxiBLAS.ldiv(F, b); y = similar(b); OxiBLAS.ldiv!(y, A, b)\nx1 ≈ x2 ≈ y\ntrue\n\nDimensions. B has size(F, 1) rows and Y has size(F, 2).\nRectangular systems. A tall system (m > n) gives the least-squares solution; a wide dense system (n > m) gives the minimum-norm solution.\nIn-place solve. Y === B is allowed for a square system. A partial overlap between Y and B is refused.\nRight-hand side and destination. A sparse B is read through a dense copy. A sparse destination is refused: the solution is dense.\nPrecision. The solve runs in the element type of the factorization. A real factor and a complex B of the same precision are solved as two real systems.","title":"Linear solves: ldiv, ldiv! and \\"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/highlevel/#Zero-allocation-rectangular-solves-and-the-workspace-keyword","page":"High-level API","text":"Unlike LinearAlgebra.ldiv!(Y, F, B), OxiBLAS.ldiv! accepts an optional workspace keyword argument:\n\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, F, B; workspace=nothing)\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, A, B; workspace=nothing)\n\nWhy workspace exists. On a rectangular factorization F of size m × n, the underlying LinearAlgebra.ldiv!(F, X) primitive operates in place on a buffer X of max(m, n) rows (Qᴴ is applied to the m rows of B, and the n solution rows are written into the leading n rows of X). When m > n (tall system), Y only has n rows and B must not be modified, so an m-row buffer is needed.\nWhen workspace is NOT needed (workspace=nothing already avoids allocating X):\nSquare systems (m == n): B is copied directly into Y and solved in place in Y (0 allocations for sparse Cholesky and dense LU/Cholesky).\nWide systems (n > m, e.g. underdetermined LQ or QR): Y has n > m rows, so OxiBLAS.ldiv! uses Y itself as the working buffer.\nSVD factorizations (any shape m × n): OxiBLAS.ldiv! projects Uᴴ B into a small rank-sized vector/matrix and writes V (Σ⁻¹ Uᴴ B) directly into Y.\nSparse right-hand sides (B sparse, m ≥ n): the dense copy of B is reused in place as the working buffer.\nWhen and how to use workspace: When solving repeated tall least-squares problems (m > n) in a loop with a dense B and QR, QRPivoted, QRSparse, or LQ', preallocate a buffer of at least m rows once per task and pass it as workspace:\n\nTall = randn(200, 80); F_qr = OxiBLAS.qr(Tall)\nb = randn(200); y = zeros(80)\nws = zeros(200)                              # allocated once per task (length >= m)\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(y, F_qr, b; workspace=ws)      # 0 allocations\ny ≈ Tall \\ b\ntrue\n\nworkspace must have 1-based indexing, size at least (max(m, n), size(B, 2)),   must not alias Y or B, and must be exclusive to the calling task during   the ldiv! call.","title":"Zero-allocation rectangular solves and the workspace keyword"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/highlevel/#Keyword-extensions-beyond-LinearAlgebra","page":"High-level API","text":"Where an OxiBLAS high-level function accepts keyword arguments beyond standard LinearAlgebra (or unifies SuiteSparse/CHOLMOD keywords on native engines), their meaning and usage are:\n\nFunction Keyword(s) Purpose and when to use\nOxiBLAS.ldiv!(Y, F_or_A, B; workspace=nothing) workspace Caller-owned buffer of size ≥ (max(m, n), size(B, 2)) for tall (m > n) QR / QRPivoted / QRSparse / LQ' solves with dense B, avoiding the m-row temporary allocation in hot loops.\nOxiBLAS.qr(A::sparse; ordering=:colamd) ordering Fill-reducing column permutation for multifrontal sparse QR: :colamd (default), :amd (AMD of AᵀA), :natural (1:n), or a custom permutation vector of 1:n.\nOxiBLAS.cholesky(A::sparse; check=true, perm=nothing, shift=0) perm, shift perm: custom fill-reducing permutation vector (nothing uses AMD). shift: diagonal shift σ factoring A + σI (as in CHOLMOD). cholesky!(F, A; check=true, shift=0) also accepts shift while reusing F.p.\nOxiBLAS.ldlt(A::sparse; perm=nothing, shift=0) perm, shift Same perm and shift options as OxiBLAS.cholesky for pivotless multifrontal LDLᵀ; ldlt!(F, A; shift=0) reuses F.p.\nOxiBLAS.bunchkaufman(A::sparse, rook=false; check=true, perm=nothing) perm Custom fill-reducing permutation vector (nothing uses AMD) for sparse indefinite Bunch–Kaufman factorization.","title":"Keyword extensions beyond LinearAlgebra"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/highlevel/#What-OxiBLAS.factorize-chooses","page":"High-level API","text":"Input Factorization\ndense square Hermitian Cholesky, or Bunch–Kaufman if it is not positive definite\ndense square LU\ndense rectangular column-pivoted QR\nsparse triangular (tested in O(nnz)) native sparse substitution\nsparse Hermitian Cholesky, or Bunch–Kaufman\nsparse complex symmetric Bunch–Kaufman\nsparse square multifrontal LU\nsparse tall multifrontal QR\nDiagonal, Bidiagonal, triangular, banded, packed structured engines (guide)","title":"What OxiBLAS.factorize chooses"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/highlevel/#Limitations","page":"High-level API","text":"Sparse systems.\nA wide (underdetermined) sparse system is not solved: the sparse QR requires m ≥ n.\nThe sparse QR is not rank-revealing: F \\ b raises SingularException when R is numerically rank-deficient.\nNo failure state. The sparse engines (LU, Cholesky) and the dense LDLᵀ raise the standard exception even with check=false.\nRefused options.\nbunchkaufman(A, true) (rook pivoting) is not available.\nAn indefinite PackedMatrix has no indefinite factorization.\nNo solve. Eigen, Schur and Hessenberg have no solve primitive, as in LinearAlgebra.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/blas/#BLAS-1,-2,-3-and-GEMM","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Sources: src/blas/.","title":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.GemmConfig","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Cache blocking. Defaults adapt upstream MC=576, KC=448/896, NC≈2048.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.GemmConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.GemmWorkspace","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"GemmWorkspace(T, m, n, k; config=GemmConfig(T), workers=Threads.nthreads())\n\nReusable panels padded toward 128-byte alignment, one pair per worker. SIMD loads require only natural real-scalar alignment, never user-array alignment. A workspace serializes concurrent calls that share it; separate workspaces run independently. Task migration never changes buffer ownership. Reconstruct if dimensions/config grow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.GemmWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.GenericWorkspace","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Reusable tile accumulation buffers for extended GEMM. A shared context is serialized; different worker buffers belong to different tasks. Float16 uses Float32 accumulators. Arbitrary floating types never enter native SIMD pointer kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.GenericWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.RotmParams","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Modified Givens parameters in BLAS order (flag,h11,h21,h12,h22). Flags -2/-1/0/1 select identity/full/unit-diagonal/implicit-off-diagonal forms. Ignored fields need not be initialized. Compatible AbstractFloat scalars use their own arithmetic; native real floats retain SIMD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.RotmParams"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.asum","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"asum(x)\n\nBLAS asum: Σ |x[i]| for real vectors, Σ (|re x[i]| + |im x[i]|) for complex ones (the BLAS convention, not the 1-norm of complex numbers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.asum"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.asum_parallel","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"asum_parallel(x; threaded=true, parallel_threshold=nothing)\n\nMultithreaded asum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.asum_parallel"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.axpy!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"axpy!(y, x, α=1) -> y\n\ny ← α·x + y in place. x and y have the same length; y must not alias x partially.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.axpy!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.axpy_parallel!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"axpy_parallel!(y, x, α=1; threaded=true, parallel_threshold=nothing) -> y\n\nMultithreaded axpy!: the vector is split into contiguous chunks above parallel_threshold elements (default: a per-type threshold).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.axpy_parallel!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.copy_vector!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"copy_vector!(y, x) -> y\n\nBLAS copy: y ← x, with a length check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.copy_vector!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.dot_kahan","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"dot_kahan(x, y; conjugate=false)\n\nDot product with Kahan compensated summation: an error bound independent of the length for well-scaled data, at about twice the cost of dotu. conjugate=true conjugates x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.dot_kahan"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.dot_pairwise","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"dot_pairwise(x, y; conjugate=false)\n\nDot product with pairwise (cascade) summation: error growth in O(log n) instead of O(n). conjugate=true conjugates x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.dot_pairwise"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.dot_parallel","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"dot_parallel(x, y; conjugate=false, threaded=true, parallel_threshold=nothing)\n\nMultithreaded dot product: each task reduces one chunk with the sequential kernel, then the partial sums are added. The result can differ from dotu/dotc by rounding (different summation order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.dot_parallel"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.dotc","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"dotc(x, y)\n\nConjugated dot product Σ conj(x[i])·y[i] (BLAS dotc), the Euclidean inner product of complex vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.dotc"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.dotu","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"dotu(x, y)\n\nUnconjugated dot product Σ x[i]·y[i] (BLAS dotu). For real vectors it equals dotc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.dotu"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.dsdot","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Float32-input inner product, products and accumulation in Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.dsdot"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"gemm!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; transA=:N, transB=:N, algorithm=:auto,\n      kernel=:auto, threaded=true, workspace=nothing, config=GemmConfig(eltype(C)))\n\nCompute C ← α op(A) op(B) + β C in native Julia. Equal Float32/64 or ComplexF32/64 element types; 1-based matrices and views. Overlapping inputs are snapshotted before writing C. β=0 never reads old C; α=0 or K=0 never reads A/B. Algorithms: reference, blocked, packed, register, simd, auto. Explicit unavailable ISA requests throw before changing C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm_batched!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Independent batched GEMM with cross-batch alias snapshots and task-owned panels. threaded=false disables the outer schedule; no nested GEMM tasks are created. A homogeneous batch may reuse one GemmWorkspace sized for its largest members.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_batched!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm_blocked!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"gemm_blocked!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; kwargs...) -> C\n\ngemm! with algorithm=:blocked: cache blocking without packing or SIMD micro-kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_blocked!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm_cache_oblivious!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Cache-oblivious largest-dimension recursion with native SIMD leaf kernels. Parallel output regions have task-owned packing panels and no nested tasks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_cache_oblivious!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm_reference!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"gemm_reference!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; kwargs...) -> C\n\ngemm! with algorithm=:reference: the plain triple loop, used as a correctness reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_reference!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm_strassen!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Native Strassen seven-product recursion, including odd-dimension fringes. This trades additional workspace and rounding error for fewer products; select it explicitly after checking the application's accuracy and performance needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_strassen!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm_strided_batched!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"GEMM on 3-D Julia arrays; dimension three is the independent batch index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_strided_batched!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemm_winograd!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Native Strassen–Winograd addition schedule with seven recursive products.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemm_winograd!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemmt!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"gemmt!(C,A,B,α=1,β=0;uplo=:U,transA=:N,transB=:N,blocksize=128,...)\n\nC[triangle] ← α op(A) op(B) + β C[triangle], preserving the other triangle. Rectangular inputs, N/T/C, alias snapshots and GEMM options are supported. Only diagonal tiles use temporary output storage, bounded by blocksize².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemmt!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gemv!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"gemv!(y, A, x, α=1, β=0; trans=:N, threaded=true, parallel_threshold=nothing) -> y\n\nGeneral matrix–vector product y ← α·op(A)·x + β·y, with op given by trans: :N (A), :T (transpose) or :C (adjoint). β = 0 never reads y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gemv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.ger!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"ger!(A, x, y, α=1) -> A\n\nRank-1 update A ← α·x·yᵀ + A (BLAS geru).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ger!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.gerc!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"gerc!(A, x, y, α=1) -> A\n\nConjugated rank-1 update A ← α·x·yᴴ + A (BLAS gerc).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gerc!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.hemm!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"hemm!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; side=:L, uplo=:U, threaded=true) -> C\n\nHermitian counterpart of symm!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hemm!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.hemv!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"hemv!(y, A, x, α=1, β=0; uplo=:U, threaded=true) -> y\n\nHermitian matrix–vector product y ← α·A·x + β·y, reading only the uplo triangle of A; the imaginary part of the diagonal is ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hemv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.her!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"her!(A, x, α=1; uplo=:U) -> A\n\nHermitian rank-1 update A ← α·x·xᴴ + A of the uplo triangle, α real.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.her!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.her2!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"her2!(A, x, y, α=1; uplo=:U) -> A\n\nHermitian rank-2 update A ← α·x·yᴴ + conj(α)·y·xᴴ + A of the uplo triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.her2!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.her2k!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"her2k!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; uplo=:U, trans=:N, threaded=true) -> C\n\nHermitian rank-2k update C ← α·A·Bᴴ + conj(α)·B·Aᴴ + β·C of the uplo triangle, β real.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.her2k!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.herk!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"herk!(C, A, α=1, β=0; uplo=:U, trans=:N, threaded=true) -> C\n\nHermitian rank-k update C ← α·A·Aᴴ + β·C (trans=:N) or α·Aᴴ·A + β·C (trans=:C) of the uplo triangle; α and β are real.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.herk!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.iamax","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"iamax(x) -> Int\n\nIndex of the first entry of largest magnitude (|re| + |im| for complex, as BLAS i?amax). Returns 0 for an empty vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.iamax"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.iamin","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"iamin(x) -> Int\n\nIndex of the first entry of smallest magnitude, with the iamax convention. Returns 0 for an empty vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.iamin"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.nrm2","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Euclidean norm with scaled sum of squares; avoids spurious overflow/underflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.nrm2"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.nrm2_parallel","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"nrm2_parallel(x; threaded=true, parallel_threshold=nothing)\n\nMultithreaded Euclidean norm with overflow-safe scaling, like nrm2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.nrm2_parallel"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.rot!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"rot!(x, y, c, s) -> (x, y)\n\nApplies the plane rotation [c s; -conj(s) c] to the pairs (x[i], y[i]) (BLAS rot), c real. The rotation itself comes from rotg.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.rot!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.rotg","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Return (c,s,r), ca+sb=r and cb-conj(s)a=0 (real c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.rotg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.rotm!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"rotm!(x,y,p::RotmParams)  # or five-element BLAS parameter vector\n\nApply the modified Givens matrix to pairs (x[i],y[i]) in native Julia. Negative/nonunit strides are supported. Nonidentity rotations require disjoint outputs with distinct entries. Identity never reads or writes vector entries. Parameter vectors are read before any mutation, including when they alias x/y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.rotm!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.rotmg","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"rotmg(d1, d2, x1, y1) -> (; d1, d2, x1, params)\n\nConstructs a modified Givens rotation (BLAS rotmg) that zeroes the second component of (√d1·x1, √d2·y1). Returns the updated scale factors d1, d2, the updated x1 and the rotation params::RotmParams (flag and h entries), which rotm! applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.rotmg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.rotmg!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"rotmg!(state,y1) updates [d1,d2,x1] after successful generation and returns parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.rotmg!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.scal!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"scal!(x, α) -> x\n\nx ← α·x in place. With α = 0 the result is exactly zero (NaN and Inf in x are not propagated), as in reference BLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.scal!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.scal_parallel!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"scal_parallel!(x, α; threaded=true, parallel_threshold=nothing) -> x\n\nMultithreaded scal!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.scal_parallel!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.sdsdot","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"sdsdot(alpha::Float32,x,y) accumulates alpha and Float32 products in Float64, then rounds once to Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sdsdot"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.swap!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"swap!(x, y) -> (x, y)\n\nBLAS swap: exchanges the contents of x and y (same length).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.swap!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.symm!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"symm!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; side=:L, uplo=:U, threaded=true) -> C\n\nSymmetric matrix product: C ← α·A·B + β·C (side=:L) or C ← α·B·A + β·C (side=:R), with A symmetric and only its uplo triangle read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.symm!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.symv!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"symv!(y, A, x, α=1, β=0; uplo=:U, threaded=true) -> y\n\nSymmetric matrix–vector product y ← α·A·x + β·y, reading only the uplo triangle (:U or :L) of A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.symv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.syr!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"syr!(A, x, α=1; uplo=:U) -> A\n\nSymmetric rank-1 update A ← α·x·xᵀ + A of the uplo triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.syr!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.syr2!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"syr2!(A, x, y, α=1; uplo=:U) -> A\n\nSymmetric rank-2 update A ← α·x·yᵀ + α·y·xᵀ + A of the uplo triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.syr2!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.syr2k!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"syr2k!(C, A, B, α=1, β=0; uplo=:U, trans=:N, threaded=true) -> C\n\nSymmetric rank-2k update C ← α·A·Bᵀ + α·B·Aᵀ + β·C (trans=:N) of the uplo triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.syr2k!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.syrk!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"syrk!(C, A, α=1, β=0; uplo=:U, trans=:N, threaded=true) -> C\n\nSymmetric rank-k update of the uplo triangle of C: C ← α·A·Aᵀ + β·C (trans=:N) or C ← α·Aᵀ·A + β·C (trans=:T). The other triangle is not written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.syrk!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.triangular_workspace","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Reusable native TRMM/TRSM packing context for an m-by-n output. Construct once for a fixed side/configuration; pass as workspace with the same config. The scalar/generic fallback requires no packing context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.triangular_workspace"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.trmm!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"In-place triangular multiplication; recursive blocks use native GEMM. blocksize sets the scalar leaf size. GEMM options control off-diagonal updates. Only aliased A requires a full logical snapshot; B is never fully copied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trmm!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.trmv!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"trmv!(x, A; uplo=:U, trans=:N, unit=false) -> x\n\nTriangular matrix–vector product x ← op(A)·x in place, A upper (uplo=:U) or lower triangular; unit=true assumes a unit diagonal without reading it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trmv!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.trsm!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"Triangular solve with recursive native-GEMM updates and reusable panels. A singular nonunit diagonal is reported before any mutation of B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trsm!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/blas/#OxiBLAS.trsv!","page":"BLAS 1, 2, 3 and GEMM","text":"trsv!(x, A; uplo=:U, trans=:N, unit=false) -> x\n\nTriangular solve op(A)·x = b in place (x holds b on entry). A zero diagonal entry raises SingularException (unless unit=true).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trsv!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Sparse-matrices","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"","title":"Sparse matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Formats","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Format Constructor\nCSRMatrix, CSCMatrix, COOMatrix CSRMatrix(m, n, rowptr, colind, values), or conversion of a matrix (CSRMatrix(S) for S::SparseMatrixCSC)\nDIAMatrix, ELLMatrix, BSRMatrix, BSCMatrix, HYBMatrix, SELLMatrix extended formats, converted from CSR/CSC\nSparseTensor N-dimensional sparse array (tensors)\n\nThese types are SparseArrays.AbstractSparseMatrix. They interoperate with SparseArrays:\n\nconversions SparseMatrixCSC(A) and CSCMatrix(S) (no copy from a SparseMatrixCSC);\nnnz, findnz, getindex, broadcast;\nhcat, vcat, kron, blockdiag, fkeep!.\n\nOxiBLAS.spdiagm and OxiBLAS.sparsevec build the OxiBLAS formats directly.","title":"Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Products","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_mul!(y, A, x, α, β; trans) computes the sparse × dense product, sparse_matmul(A, B) the sparse × sparse product. A * B, A * x and OxiBLAS.mul! dispatch to the same kernels.","title":"Products"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Solves:-\\-and-factorizations","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"using OxiBLAS, SparseArrays, LinearAlgebra\nn = 400; S = sprand(n, n, 0.01) + 10I; A = CSRMatrix(S); b = randn(n)\nx = A \\ b                         # factorization chosen from the structure\nF = OxiBLAS.lu(A)                 # multifrontal LU, F.L*F.U == A[F.p,F.q]\nP = CSRMatrix(S * S' + I)\nC = OxiBLAS.cholesky(Hermitian(P))\n(norm(S * x - b) / norm(b), norm(S * (F \\ b) - b) / norm(b), norm(P * (C \\ b) - b) / norm(b))\n(1.8059449601164365e-16, 1.8059449601164365e-16, 6.721346056175582e-16)\n\nA \\ B on an OxiBLAS format follows the order of SparseArrays:\n\nStructure (tested in O(nnz)) Factorization\ntriangular or diagonal native sparse substitution (sparse_triangular_solve)\nHermitian multifrontal Cholesky, or Bunch–Kaufman if not positive definite\ncomplex symmetric Bunch–Kaufman\nother square multifrontal LU\ntall multifrontal QR (least squares)\n\nEngine Functions\nLU multifrontal_lu, sparse_lu, sparse_lu_static, sparse_lu_blocked\nCholesky multifrontal_cholesky, supernodal_cholesky, sparse_cholesky, symbolic_cholesky\nindefinite LDLᵀ sparse_ldlt (Bunch–Kaufman within fronts), ldl_inertia\nQR sparse_qr, sparse_apply_q!\nout of core out_of_core_lu, out_of_core_cholesky, out_of_core_memory_usage\ntriangular sparse_triangular_solve","title":"Solves: \\ and factorizations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Iterative-solvers-and-preconditioners","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Examples, the table of which solver suits which matrix, and the result fields: How do I… solve iteratively.\n\nSolvers. cg, pcg, gmres, fgmres, bicgstab, minres, tfqmr, qmr, idrs, and their block variants block_cg and block_gmres. Each returns the solution, the convergence status, the iterations and the actually recomputed residual.\nPreconditioners. ilu0, ilut, ic0, ict, spai, ainv, amg, relaxation_preconditioner, polynomial_preconditioner and additive_schwarz.","title":"Iterative solvers and preconditioners"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Orderings-and-graphs","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"The available ordering families:\n\nminimum degree: amd_ordering, colamd_ordering, minimum_degree_ordering, multiple_minimum_degree, column_minimum_degree;\nprofile and bandwidth: reverse_cuthill_mckee;\nnested dissection: nested_dissection, multilevel_nested_dissection.\n\nThe graph tools are:\n\nconnected_components, level_sets, multilevel_partition, refine_partition;\nbipartite matchings (bipartite_matching, weighted_bipartite_matching);\nchoice of an ordering: sparse_ordering, select_sparse_ordering, ordering_metrics, compare_orderings.","title":"Orderings and graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Input-and-output","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"read_matrix_market and write_matrix_market read and write the Matrix Market format.","title":"Input and output"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/sparse/#Limitations","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Wide (underdetermined) sparse systems. There is no solver: OxiBLAS.qr refuses a wide matrix with an ArgumentError, and so does A \\ b.\nSparse QR without rank detection. It is not rank-revealing: SingularException on a numerically rank-deficient matrix.\nSparse destination refused. A solution is dense.\nInverse refused. OxiBLAS.inv of a sparse matrix raises an ArgumentError: factorize and solve instead.\nNo failure state. The sparse LU and Cholesky engines raise the standard exception even with check=false.\nSparseMatrixCSC unchanged. OxiBLAS adds no LinearAlgebra/ SparseArrays method on this type: S \\ b stays the one of SparseArrays (UMFPACK, CHOLMOD). OxiBLAS.ldiv(S, b) and OxiBLAS.lu(S) use the OxiBLAS engines.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/sparse/#Sparse-matrices","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Sources: src/sparse/.","title":"Sparse matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS-methods-of-other-modules'-functions","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"These functions belong to LinearAlgebra, SparseArrays or Base; OxiBLAS adds methods for its own types to them (see the guides):\n\nLinearAlgebra.diag, LinearAlgebra.dot, SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC, SparseArrays.SparseVector","title":"OxiBLAS methods of other modules' functions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.AINVPreconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Factored sparse approximate inverse ZDiagonal(1/d)W'. Biconjugation without pivoting; a zero/nonfinite pivot is reported explicitly. Dropping is relative to each updated column and retains its unit diagonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.AINVPreconditioner"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.AMGPreconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"AMGPreconditioner{T,R}\n\nResult of amg: an algebraic multigrid hierarchy (aggregation, optionally smoothed) applied as one V- or W-cycle per preconditioner call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.AMGPreconditioner"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.AdditiveSchwarzPreconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"AdditiveSchwarzPreconditioner{T,R,F}\n\nResult of additive_schwarz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.AdditiveSchwarzPreconditioner"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ApproxInversePreconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"ApproxInversePreconditioner{T}\n\nResult of spai: an explicit sparse approximate inverse M ≈ A⁻¹ (field inverse), applied as z = M·r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ApproxInversePreconditioner"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.BSCMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"BSCMatrix(A::CSRMatrix; blocksize=(4,4))\n\nBlock sparse column storage (the transpose of a BSRMatrix layout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BSCMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.BSRMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"BSRMatrix(A::CSRMatrix; blocksize=(4,4))\nBSRMatrix(m, n, br, bc, rowptr, colind, blocks)\n\nBlock sparse row storage: dense br×bc blocks (blocks[:,:,k]) indexed like a CSR matrix of blocks. Suited to matrices from vector-valued PDEs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BSRMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.COOMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Canonical coordinate sparse storage. Duplicate coordinates are summed and exact zero entries are removed during construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.COOMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.CSCMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Owned compressed-column sparse matrix with validated 1-based indices.\n\nA SparseArrays.AbstractSparseMatrix, like the other OxiBLAS formats (not an AbstractSparseMatrixCSC: operations on OxiBLAS sparse types end in OxiBLAS kernels, not in inherited SparseArrays ones; see reports/ARCHITECTURECSCAvsB.md). Row indices are unique and sorted within each column, so SparseMatrixCSC(A) always shares the storage; explicit stored zeros are allowed, as in SparseMatrixCSC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.CSCMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.CSRMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Owned CSR matrix. Indices are 1-based; duplicate columns in a row are rejected. Real, complex and generic (e.g. BigFloat) scalars; explicit zeros are kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.CSRMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.DIAMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"DIAMatrix(A::CSRMatrix)\nDIAMatrix(m, n, offsets, data)\n\nDiagonal storage: data[i,k] is the entry (i, i+offsets[k]). Efficient for matrices with a few full diagonals (stencils). Convertible from any OxiBLAS sparse format or SparseMatrixCSC; usable with sparse_mul! and the iterative solvers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.DIAMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ELLMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"ELLMatrix(A::CSRMatrix; width=max row length)\nELLMatrix(m, n, indices, values)\n\nELLPACK storage: every row padded to width entries (indices[i,k], values[i,k], padding with index 0). Regular access pattern for matrices with rows of similar length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ELLMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.HYBMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"HYBMatrix(A::CSRMatrix; width=average row length)\n\nHybrid storage: an ELLMatrix part of fixed width plus a COOMatrix for the entries of the longer rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.HYBMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ILUFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"ILUFactor{T}\n\nResult of ilu0: incomplete LU with zero fill (the sparsity pattern of A). A preconditioner for gmres, bicgstab…\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ILUFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.MultifrontalCholeskyFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"MultifrontalCholeskyFactor{T}\n\nResult of multifrontal_cholesky / supernodal_cholesky: A[p,p] = L·Lᴴ with the elimination tree (parents) and the supernodes (nodes). The engine behind OxiBLAS.cholesky on sparse matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.MultifrontalCholeskyFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.MultifrontalLUFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"MultifrontalLUFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of multifrontal_lu: A[p,q] = L·U computed front by front along the elimination tree, with threshold partial pivoting (pivot_threshold) and the observed growth_factor. The engine behind OxiBLAS.lu and \\ on sparse matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.MultifrontalLUFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.OutOfCoreFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"OutOfCoreFactor{T}\n\nResult of out_of_core_lu / out_of_core_cholesky: a dense factorization whose tiles live in a file, with at most memory_budget bytes in memory. Solve with solve; out_of_core_memory_usage reports the memory used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.OutOfCoreFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.PolynomialPreconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"PolynomialPreconditioner{T,R}\n\nResult of polynomial_preconditioner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.PolynomialPreconditioner"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.RelaxationPreconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"RelaxationPreconditioner{T,R}\n\nResult of relaxation_preconditioner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.RelaxationPreconditioner"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.SELLMatrix","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"SELLMatrix(A::CSRMatrix; sliceheight=8, sorting_window=1)\n\nSliced ELLPACK (SELL-C-σ): rows grouped in slices of sliceheight, each padded to its own width, optionally sorted by length within windows of sorting_window slices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SELLMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.SparseBlockedLUFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"SparseBlockedLUFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of sparse_lu_blocked: a SparseLUFactor computed by dense panels. Solve with solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseBlockedLUFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.SparseCholeskyFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"SparseCholeskyFactor{T,R}\n\nSparse Cholesky A = L·Lᴴ (CSC factor), from sparse_cholesky or the incomplete ic0/ict (incomplete = true). Solve with solve; usable as a preconditioner for pcg.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseCholeskyFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.SparseLDLFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"SparseLDLFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of sparse_ldlt: sparse symmetric indefinite factorization A[p,p] = L·D·Lᴴ with 1×1 and 2×2 pivots. Solve with solve; ldl_inertia counts the positive, negative and zero eigenvalues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseLDLFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.SparseLUFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"SparseLUFactor{T,R}\n\nSparse LU A[p,q] = L·U (CSR factors), from sparse_lu or the incomplete ilut (drop_tolerance > 0). Solve with solve; usable as a preconditioner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseLUFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.SparseQRFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"SparseQRFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of sparse_qr: sparse Householder QR A[:,p] = Q·R, with Q kept as sparse reflectors. solve / least_squares give the least-squares solution; sparse_apply_q! applies Q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseQRFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.SparseStaticLUFactor","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"SparseStaticLUFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of sparse_lu_static: LU with static pivoting, where tiny pivots are replaced by ±epsilon (perturbations records them) instead of being exchanged. Pair it with iterative refinement; sparse_direct_diagnostics reports whether it was perturbed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SparseStaticLUFactor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.additive_schwarz","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"additive_schwarz(A; subdomains=nthreads(), overlap=1, solver=:cholesky, threaded=true) -> AdditiveSchwarzPreconditioner\n\nOverlapping additive Schwarz (domain decomposition): A is split into subdomains contiguous blocks extended by overlap rows; each local block is factorized (solver = :cholesky, :lu or :ilu0) and the local solves are added, in parallel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.additive_schwarz"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ainv","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"ainv(A; droptol=0.1, maxnnz=20) -> AINVPreconditioner\n\nApproximate inverse preconditioner by biconjugation (AINV): sparse factors Z·D⁻¹·Wᴴ ≈ A⁻¹, applied by sparse products only (no triangular solves, hence parallel).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ainv"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.amd_ordering","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"amd_ordering(A; kind=:symmetric)\n\nApproximate minimum degree ordering of A+Aᵀ (kind=:symmetric, for Cholesky and LDLᵀ), of AᵀA (:ata, for QR) or of AᵀA without dense rows (:ata_sparse, for LU). Returns p with p[new] = old.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.amd_ordering"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.amg","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Aggregation algebraic multigrid for Hermitian positive-definite sparse A. Piecewise-constant aggregates may be smoothed before Galerkin coarsening. V/W cycles use weighted Jacobi and a native sparse Cholesky coarse solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.amg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.bandwidth_profile","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"bandwidth_profile(A) -> (; lower, upper, bandwidth, profile)\n\nLower and upper bandwidths, total bandwidth lower + upper + 1 and profile (envelope size) of A, counting the stored nonzero entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bandwidth_profile"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.bicgstab","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"BiCGStab for real/complex nonsymmetric systems with right preconditioning. Reports true residuals and explicit breakdown/maxiter termination.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bicgstab"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.bipartite_matching","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Maximum-cardinality row/column matching by layered, iterative Hopcroft–Karp. Returns row/column partner arrays (0 means unmatched), retaining the existing rectangular incidence interpretation. Use bipartitegraphmatching for a square adjacency graph interpreted through an undirected two-coloring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bipartite_matching"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.block_cg","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Coupled block preconditioned CG for multiple Hermitian positive-definite systems. Rank loss in the block Gram system triggers explicit scalar PCG continuation from the current iterates, rather than inverting a singular block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.block_cg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.block_gmres","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Restarted right-preconditioned block GMRES with block Arnoldi and native minimum-norm projected least squares. Rank loss falls back to independent flexible GMRES continuations; the returned fallback flag makes this explicit. maxiter counts block Arnoldi steps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.block_gmres"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.cg","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Conjugate gradient for Hermitian positive definite matrices; optional positive real Jacobi diagonal. Reports the true residual and a termination reason.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.cg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.colamd_ordering","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"colamd_ordering(A)\n\nColumn approximate minimum degree ordering of A (COLAMD, ported from SuiteSparse): a column permutation q (q[new] = old) that keeps the factors of AᵀA, the R of A[:,q] = QR and the L, U of A[p,q] = LU sparse. Stored zeros are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.colamd_ordering"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.connected_components","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"connected_components(A) -> (; labels, components, count)\n\nConnected components of the (symmetrized) graph of A: labels[i] is the component of vertex i, components lists the vertices of each one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.connected_components"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.fgmres","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Flexible right-preconditioned restarted GMRES. A callable preconditioner has signature P(z,r,iteration), so it may change between Arnoldi steps. Stored preconditioned basis vectors make this flexibility mathematically valid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.fgmres"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.gmres","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Restarted, left-preconditioned GMRES with two-pass modified Gram–Schmidt. Convergence is checked against the true, unpreconditioned residual every restart. preconditioner may be nothing, a diagonal vector, or an ILUFactor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.gmres"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ic0","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"ic0(A; kwargs...) -> SparseCholeskyFactor\n\nIncomplete Cholesky with zero fill, for Hermitian positive definite A: the usual preconditioner of pcg.\n\nx = pcg(A, b; preconditioner=ic0(A)).x\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ic0"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ict","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"ict(A; droptol=1e-3, kwargs...) -> SparseCholeskyFactor\n\nIncomplete Cholesky with threshold dropping (more fill than ic0, fewer iterations).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ict"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.idrs","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"IDR(s) with an orthonormal local shadow space, oblique Sonneveld-space updates, angle-stabilized omega and optional fixed right preconditioning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.idrs"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ilu0","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Zero-fill incomplete LU on the input sparsity pattern. Requires explicit, nonzero pivots; numerical breakdown is reported rather than silently patched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ilu0"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ilut","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"ilut(A; droptol=1e-3, maxfill=20, kwargs...) -> SparseLUFactor\n\nIncomplete LU with threshold dropping: entries below droptol (relative to their row) are dropped and each row keeps at most maxfill extra entries. Stronger than ilu0 for nonsymmetric systems.\n\nx = gmres(A, b; preconditioner=ilut(A)).x\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ilut"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.lanczos","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Fully reorthogonalized Hermitian Lanczos projection and Ritz eigenpairs. Returns explicit residuals and convergence flags. which is :smallest or :largest algebraic. A supplied start vector determines the explored subspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lanczos"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.ldl_inertia","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Inertia from real/Hermitian pivot blocks in working precision. tol applies to D-block eigenvalues, not to eigenvalues of A or a certified spectral gap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ldl_inertia"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.level_sets","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"level_sets(A, start) -> Vector{Vector{Int}}\n\nBreadth-first level structure of the graph of A from vertex start (level 1 is [start]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.level_sets"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.minimum_degree_ordering","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"minimum_degree_ordering(A) -> Vector{Int}\n\nExact minimum degree ordering on the quotient graph (supervariables, mass elimination): a fill-reducing permutation for sparse Cholesky and LU. See also amd_ordering (approximate, faster) and multiple_minimum_degree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.minimum_degree_ordering"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.minres","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Paige–Saunders short-recurrence MINRES for Hermitian systems, including indefinite matrices. The optional fixed preconditioner must be Hermitian positive definite. Every reported convergence uses the true residual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.minres"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.multifrontal_cholesky","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Supernodal multifrontal Hermitian Cholesky of the uplo triangle of A, P·A·Pᵀ = L·Lᴴ, with a fill-reducing ordering (AMD by default). The dense fronts use this package's POTRF/TRSM/HERK (threaded lets them use threads). supernodal=:auto follows CHOLMOD: an up-looking simplicial factorization when flops/nnz(L) < 40 (chain-like factors), supernodal otherwise; true or false force one of them. maxwidth caps the supernode width; uncapped by default, as in CHOLMOD (a cap of 64 split the 3D separators and ran HERK at rank 64: ×1.35 against CHOLMOD on a 40³ Laplacian instead of ×1.10).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.multifrontal_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.multifrontal_lu","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Row-element multifrontal LU with separate row and column permutations. A[p,q]=L*U. Original row elements and child Schur contributions are assembled once, factored in fully summed panels and forwarded through an assembly tree. Threshold partial row pivoting searches all candidate rows of the front. Independent fronts run concurrently; inner TRSM/GEMM disable nested threading. No dense global Schur matrix or wrapper around blocked sparse LU is used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.multifrontal_lu"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.nested_dissection","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"BFS level-separator nested dissection, with exact local minimum-degree leaves, disconnected-component handling and an explicit recursion depth cap. Use return_tree=true to inspect separators and achieved size balance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.nested_dissection"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.out_of_core_cholesky","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Native tiled disk Hermitian Cholesky, with the same cache/budget contract as outofcore_lu. Only uplo is read. Cache files are local native-endian tiles, checksummed and shape/type checked; they are not portable persistent checkpoints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.out_of_core_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.out_of_core_lu","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Native tiled disk LU with global panel row pivoting. The byte budget covers numeric panel/tile/packing payload, not CSR input, returned solutions, metadata or process RSS. Tile size shrinks to fit; an insufficient budget is rejected. Call close on the returned owned cache when finished; GC cleanup is best effort.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.out_of_core_lu"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.out_of_core_memory_usage","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"out_of_core_memory_usage(F::OutOfCoreFactor) -> NamedTuple\n\nMemory accounting of an out-of-core factorization: (; payload_bytes, budget_bytes, metadata_bytes, includes_input_output, includes_runtime_overhead).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.out_of_core_memory_usage"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.pcg","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Preconditioned conjugate gradients with an explicit positive-definite preconditioner and true residual checks. Breakdowns are never convergence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.pcg"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.polynomial_preconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"polynomial_preconditioner(A; degree=8, bounds=nothing, kind=:chebyshev) -> PolynomialPreconditioner\n\nPolynomial preconditioner p(A) ≈ A⁻¹ of the given degree: Chebyshev on the spectral interval bounds = (lo, hi) (estimated by Gershgorin discs when nothing) or a truncated Neumann series (kind=:neumann). Applied by sparse products only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.polynomial_preconditioner"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.qmr","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Unpreconditioned two-sided QMR, using A and A' products and short bi-Lanczos recurrences. True residuals govern all termination decisions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qmr"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.read_matrix_market","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"read_matrix_market(path_or_io; T=nothing, format=:csr)\n\nReads a Matrix Market file (coordinate or array; real, integer, complex or pattern; general, symmetric, hermitian or skew-symmetric). Symmetric storage is expanded. T overrides the element type (default Float64, or ComplexF64 for complex files); format selects the result: :csr, :csc, :coo (OxiBLAS formats) or :dense (Matrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.read_matrix_market"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.relaxation_preconditioner","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"relaxation_preconditioner(A; omega=1, sweeps=1, symmetric=false) -> RelaxationPreconditioner\n\nRelaxation sweeps as a preconditioner: Gauss–Seidel (omega = 1), SOR (0 < omega < 2), or the symmetric SSOR (symmetric=true, required by pcg and minres). sweeps sweeps per application.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.relaxation_preconditioner"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.reverse_cuthill_mckee","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"reverse_cuthill_mckee(A) -> Vector{Int}\n\nReverse Cuthill–McKee ordering of the (symmetrized) graph of A: a permutation that reduces the bandwidth and the profile. Apply it with sparse_permute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.reverse_cuthill_mckee"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.spai","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Sparse approximate inverse from independent column least-squares problems. pattern_levels expands the initial column graph support. Each task owns its sparse QR and scratch; assembly happens after all column solves complete.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.spai"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_add","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_add(A, B, α=1, β=1) -> CSRMatrix\n\nSparse linear combination α·A + β·B (same size and element type), with duplicate entries merged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_add"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_apply_q!","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_apply_q!(B, F::SparseQRFactor; trans=:N) -> B\n\nApplies the orthogonal factor of a sparse QR to the columns of B in place: Q·B (trans=:N) or Qᴴ·B (trans=:C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_apply_q!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_cholesky","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Sparse Cholesky in the natural order, A = L·Lᴴ. Fill is stored sparsely, never in a dense matrix. The exact factor goes through the supernodal code of multifrontal_cholesky (natural ordering; the postorder is undone); fill=:zero gives IC(0) and positive droptol threshold IC, by left-looking columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_diagonal","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_diagonal(values; offset=0, m, n) -> CSRMatrix\n\nSparse matrix with values on the diagonal offset (0: main diagonal, positive: above). The size defaults to the smallest one that holds the diagonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_diagonal"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_direct_diagnostics","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_direct_diagnostics(A, F, b, x=solve(F, b)) -> NamedTuple\n\nQuality check of a sparse direct solve: (; residual_norms, componentwise_backward_error, finite, perturbed), one entry per right-hand side; perturbed is true when F is a SparseStaticLUFactor that replaced pivots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_direct_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_ldlt","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Sparse symmetric/Hermitian LDL with Bunch–Kaufman 1x1/2x2 pivots, by supernodal multifrontal elimination (src/sparse/supernodal_ldlt.jl). Only uplo is read. Factor identity: A[p,p]=LD(hermitian ? L' : transpose(L)); p is the elimination order (the ordering composed with a postorder and the pivots). pivot=:none is also supported. Exact zero pivots are errors, not perturbations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_ldlt"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_lu","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Sparse LU with threshold partial row pivoting, A[p,q] = L·U.\n\nordering (exact factor):\n\n:auto (default): as multifrontal_lu (UMFPACK's symmetric strategy for a mostly symmetric pattern, singletons and AMD(AᵀA) otherwise);\n:natural: q = 1:n, left-looking Gilbert–Peierls;\n:colamd, :amd_ata or a permutation vector: Gilbert–Peierls in that column order.\n\ndroptol>0 gives threshold incomplete LU in the natural order, with maxfill bounding the retained lower/upper entries per row, by dictionary rows. No dense expansion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_lu"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_lu_blocked","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Sparse blocked right-looking LU. Sparse pivot panels are factored in column dictionaries; only the active L21/U12 update front is packed densely for native TRSM/GEMM. Global A is never expanded to an n×n dense matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_lu_blocked"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_lu_static","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Fixed-order sparse LU with phase-preserving diagonal perturbations. A[p,q]+Diagonal(perturbations)=L*U, with p=q from a symmetric prescribed ordering. A perturbed factor solves the modified system, not exactly A. relative=true scales epsilon by the largest input magnitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_lu_static"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_matmul","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Gustavson CSR×CSR multiplication with a sparse row accumulator and sorted output rows. No dense matrix expansion or external sparse backend.\n\n\n\n\n\nSparse × sparse for SparseArrays operands, returned as a SparseMatrixCSC. (AB)ᵀ = BᵀAᵀ, and the CSR forms of Aᵀ and Bᵀ share A's and B's storage, so the Gustavson CSR product of the transposes is the CSC of AB: no input or output transposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_matmul"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_mul!","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Native CSR SpMV including transpose/adjoint and overlapping x/y snapshots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_mul!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_norm","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_norm(A, kind=:fro)\n\nNorm of a sparse matrix from its stored entries: :fro, :max, :one (column sums) or :inf (row sums).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_norm"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_permute","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_permute(A, p, q=p) -> CSRMatrix\n\nPermuted copy A[p, q] (symmetric permutation A[p, p] by default), for applying an ordering such as reverse_cuthill_mckee.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_permute"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_qr","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Sparse Householder QR, A[:,p] = Q·[R; 0].\n\npivot=true (default): rank-revealing column pivoting by recomputed norms, column by column, with fill in column dictionaries. pivot=false: multifrontal QR (src/sparse/sparseqrmultifrontal.jl) in the column ordering: :natural (p = 1:n), :amd (AMD of AᵀA), :colamd (COLAMD, SPQR's default kind of order) or a permutation vector. The input is never expanded densely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_qr"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_svd","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_svd(A, rank; oversampling=8, power_iterations=2, seed, threaded=true) -> SVDFactor\n\nRandomized partial SVD of a sparse matrix: the leading rank singular triplets from a random range finder with power_iterations refinement steps. Fast for low ranks; for an accurate partial SVD with convergence control use sparse_svd_lanczos.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_svd"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_transpose","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"sparse_transpose(A; conjugate=false) -> CSRMatrix\n\nExplicit transpose (or adjoint with conjugate=true) as a new CSR matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_transpose"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.sparse_triangular_solve","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Native sparse triangular substitution, with transpose/adjoint and unit options. The compressed storage of the operand is used as it is (CSR rows or CSC columns, a SparseMatrixCSC shared without copy); other formats are converted to CSR once. A zero pivot throws SingularException before the result is returned; the right-hand side is never modified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.sparse_triangular_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.supernodal_cholesky","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"supernodal_cholesky(A; kwargs...) -> MultifrontalCholeskyFactor\n\nmultifrontal_cholesky with supernodes forced on (dense blocks for columns with identical structure).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.supernodal_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.symbolic_cholesky","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Symbolic Cholesky pattern and elimination forest in the chosen ordering. By default the full pattern is symmetrized; uplo=:L/:U selects one triangle for the upstream symbolic selected-triangle contract. Elimination tree, children, postorder and the row structure of every factor column are computed in O(nnz(L)) (sparse_amd.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.symbolic_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.tfqmr","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"Transpose-free quasi-minimal residual with optional fixed right preconditioning. maxiter counts half-steps; the stopping residual is exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tfqmr"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/sparse/#OxiBLAS.write_matrix_market","page":"Sparse matrices","text":"write_matrix_market(path_or_io, A; symmetry=:general)\n\nWrites a sparse matrix in Matrix Market coordinate format. With symmetry=:symmetric, :hermitian or :skew (square matrices) only the lower triangle is written (strictly lower for :skew).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.write_matrix_market"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lapack/#Dense-factorizations-and-LAPACK","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Sources: src/lapack/.","title":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.BalanceFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"BalanceFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of balance_matrix: matrix = Diagonal(1 ./ scales)·A[p,p]·Diagonal(scales) (fields matrix, permutation, scales, ilo, ihi). Eigenvectors of the balanced matrix go back with balance_backtransform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BalanceFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.BidiagonalFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Compact two-sided Householder reduction. A=UBV'; B is upper bidiagonal for tall inputs and lower bidiagonal for wide inputs. Reflector storage is O(m*n). U and V are materialized only when the corresponding property is requested.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.BidiagonalFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.CholeskyFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"CholeskyFactor{T}\n\nResult of cholesky_factor: A = L·Lᴴ, L lower triangular (field L). Solve with solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.CholeskyFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.CompleteLUFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"CompleteLUFactor{T}\n\nResult of lu_complete or lu_rook: A[p,q] = L·U with row and column pivoting. Fields factors, p, q, info, pivot (:complete or :rook).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.CompleteLUFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.CompleteOrthogonalFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"CompleteOrthogonalFactor{T}\n\nResult of complete_orthogonal: A[:,p] = Q·[T 0; 0 0]·Zᴴ with the numerical rank. solve gives the minimum-norm least-squares solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.CompleteOrthogonalFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.ConvergenceError","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"ConvergenceError(algorithm, iterations, active)\n\nException raised when an iterative algorithm (QR sweeps, Jacobi, MRRR…) does not converge within its iteration budget: algorithm names it, active is the index range still unconverged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ConvergenceError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.EigenFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"EigenFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of the Hermitian eigensolvers (eigen_hermitian and variants): real values in increasing order, vectors (columns, or nothing), iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.EigenFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.HessenbergFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"HessenbergFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of hessenberg_factor: A = Q·H·Qᴴ with H upper Hessenberg, stored as reflectors (factors, tau). hessenberg_q forms Q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.HessenbergFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.LDLFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"LDLFactor{T}\n\nResult of ldlt_factor / bunchkaufman_factor: A[p,p] = L·D·Lᴴ (or L·D·Lᵀ when hermitian is false), L unit lower, D block diagonal with 1×1 and 2×2 blocks (blocks lists their sizes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.LDLFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.LUFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"LUFactor{T}\n\nResult of lu_factor: P·A = L·U with partial pivoting.\n\nfactors: L (unit lower, below the diagonal) and U (upper) packed in one matrix; F.L and F.U extract them.\npivots: LAPACK interchange sequence (row j was swapped with pivots[j]); p: the resulting permutation, A[p,:] == L*U.\ninfo: 0, or the index of the first zero pivot.\n\nSolve with solve(F, b); the high-level OxiBLAS.lu wraps it in a LinearAlgebra.Factorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.LUFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.OrthogonalFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"OrthogonalFactor{T}\n\nResult of the RQ, QL and LQ factorizations: fields triangular, orthogonal and kind (:RQ, :QL or :LQ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.OrthogonalFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.PivotedQRFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"PivotedQRFactor{T}\n\nResult of qr_pivoted: A[:,p] = Q·R with column pivoting (fields qr::QRFactor and p). Rank-revealing; solve gives the least-squares solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.PivotedQRFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.QRFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"QRFactor{T}\n\nResult of qr_factor: Householder QR in compact form (factors holds R and the reflectors, tau their scalars). F.Q/F.R extract the factors; qr_q forms Q explicitly; solve gives the least-squares solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.QRFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.QZFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"QZFactor{T,C}\n\nResult of qz_factor: generalized Schur form A = Q·S·Zᴴ, B = Q·T·Zᴴ. The generalized eigenvalues are alpha ./ beta (F.values).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.QZFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.SVDFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"SVDFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of svd_factor and the other SVD routines: A ≈ U·Diagonal(S)·Vt. U and Vt are nothing when the vectors were not requested; S is sorted in decreasing order; sweeps counts the iterations of the algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SVDFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.SchurFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"SchurFactor{T,C}\n\nResult of schur_factor: A = Z·T·Zᴴ, T quasi-upper triangular (real Schur form, 2×2 blocks for complex pairs) or upper triangular (complex), values the eigenvalues, iterations the QR sweeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SchurFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.SchurWorkspace","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"SchurWorkspace(T, n; blocksize=64, nshifts=6)\n\nReusable buffers of the multishift QR sweeps of schur_factor for matrices of order up to n. Pass it as workspace= to avoid allocating them at every call. Not shareable between concurrent calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.SchurWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.TridiagonalFactor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"TridiagonalFactor{T,R}\n\nResult of tridiagonal_factor: Hermitian A = Q·Tri·Qᴴ with Tri real symmetric tridiagonal (diag, offdiag). tridiagonal_q forms Q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.TridiagonalFactor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.apply_hessenberg_q!","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"apply_hessenberg_q!(B,F;side=:L,trans=:N,algorithm=:auto,blocksize=32)\n\nApply Q, Qᵀ or Qᴴ from compact F, on either side, without materializing Q. Vectors support left application. Algorithm :blocked uses compact WY and this package's GEMM; :unblocked applies individual reflectors. Overlap with F is snapshotted. F and B must have identical element types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.apply_hessenberg_q!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.apply_q!","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Apply a stored Householder Q on the left/right, with N/T/C semantics. Aliased factor storage is snapshotted before changing the output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.apply_q!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.backward_error","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"backward_error(A, x, b) -> (; error, residual)\n\nComponentwise backward error of an approximate solution x of A·x = b: max |b - A·x|ᵢ / (|A|·|x| + |b|)ᵢ, and the residual b - A·x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.backward_error"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.balance_backtransform","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"balance_backtransform(B::BalanceFactor, V; left=false) -> Matrix\n\nMaps eigenvectors V of the balanced matrix B.matrix back to eigenvectors of the original matrix (LAPACK gebak). left=true for left eigenvectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.balance_backtransform"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.balance_matrix","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Permutation isolation and radix-two Osborne balancing. B=Diagonal(1/scales)A[p,p]Diagonal(scales). Input is not changed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.balance_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.band_cholesky","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"band_cholesky(A::BandedMatrix) -> BandCholeskyFactor\n\nCholesky factorization of a Hermitian positive definite banded matrix, in band storage (no fill outside the band). Solve with solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.band_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.bidiagonal_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"bidiagonal_factor(A) -> BidiagonalFactor\n\nHouseholder reduction A = Q·B·Pᴴ with B upper bidiagonal (lower for a wide A, which is reduced through its adjoint): the first stage of the Golub–Kahan SVD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bidiagonal_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.bunchkaufman_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"bunchkaufman_factor(A; uplo=:L, hermitian=true) -> LDLFactor\n\nSymmetric indefinite factorization with Bunch–Kaufman pivoting (1×1 and 2×2 pivots). Same as ldlt_factor(A; pivot=:bunchkaufman).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.bunchkaufman_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.cholesky_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Blocked LLᴴ Cholesky using the lower triangle; GEMM updates native panels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.cholesky_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.cholesky_inverse","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Inverse of a Hermitian positive definite matrix from its Cholesky factor (LAPACK potri): inv(A)=inv(L)'*inv(L), returned as a full Hermitian matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.cholesky_inverse"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.column_space","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"column_space(A; rtol, atol) -> Matrix\n\nOrthonormal basis of the range of A (left singular vectors of the singular values above the tolerance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.column_space"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.complete_orthogonal","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Rank-revealing complete orthogonal decomposition: A[:,p]=Q[:,1:r]triangularZ[1:r,:]. The middle factor is lower triangular. Uses pivoted QR followed by LQ, and supports minimum-norm least squares.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.complete_orthogonal"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.condition_number","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"condition_number(A; norm=:two)\n\nCondition number ‖A‖·‖A⁻¹‖. norm=:two uses the singular values (σmax/σmin, Inf when singular, also for rectangular A); :one, :inf and :fro form the inverse explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.condition_number"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.determinant","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"determinant(A)\n\nDeterminant from an LU factorization (0 when a pivot is exactly zero). For large matrices prefer logabsdet_factor, which does not overflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.determinant"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_general","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"General eigenvectors via the real Francis / complex Wilkinson Schur path and block TREVC back/forward substitution. Real 2x2 blocks are preserved during factorization; the returned eigenvectors have one complex column per eigenvalue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_general"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_generalized","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Hermitian-definite generalized eigenproblem AV=BV*Diagonal(values). B must be positive definite. V'BV=I. Uses native Cholesky, TRSM and EVD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_generalized"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_hermitian","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"eigen_hermitian(A;uplo=:U,vectors=true,maxiter=100*max(1,size(A,1)))\n\nReal symmetric/complex Hermitian eigenpairs, ascending eigenvalues. Uses native rank-2 Householder tridiagonalization, diagonal phase correction and implicit QR. Only uplo and the real diagonal are read. vectors=false skips Q construction. Convergence failure throws ConvergenceError; no external BLAS/LAPACK backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_hermitian"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_hermitian_dc","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"eigen_hermitian_dc(A; uplo=:U, vectors=true, kwargs...) -> EigenFactor\n\nHermitian eigendecomposition by tridiagonal reduction and Cuppen divide-and-conquer: fastest for all eigenvectors of large matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_hermitian_dc"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_hermitian_mrrr","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"eigen_hermitian_mrrr(A; uplo=:U, vectors=true, indices=nothing, interval=nothing, kwargs...) -> EigenFactor\n\nHermitian eigendecomposition by tridiagonal reduction and MRRR (multiple relatively robust representations): O(n²) work for the eigenvectors, and a subset by indices (e.g. 1:10) or by an interval (lo, hi].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_hermitian_mrrr"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_pencil","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"eigen_pencil(A, B; vectors=true, left=false, kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nGeneralized eigenproblem A·x = λ·B·x for a general pencil through the QZ algorithm. Returns (; values, alpha, beta, vectors, left_vectors, qz), with values = alpha ./ beta (infinite when beta is zero). For Hermitian A and positive definite B use eigen_generalized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_pencil"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_tridiagonal","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Eigenpairs of a real symmetric tridiagonal, sorted ascending; implicit QR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_tridiagonal"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_tridiagonal_dc","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Cuppen divide-and-conquer eigenpairs of a real symmetric tridiagonal. Recursive blocks use independent storage; merges solve the secular rank-one problem, with deflation of repeated poles. vectors=false discards the vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_tridiagonal_dc"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigen_tridiagonal_mrrr","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"MRRR eigenpairs with splitting, robust shifted LDL representations, differential qd and twisted vectors. A failed representation falls back to the native implicit-QR block solver. Selected index/range output is supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigen_tridiagonal_mrrr"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.eigenvalues","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"eigenvalues(A; kwargs...) -> Vector\n\nEigenvalues of a general square matrix, from the Schur form (schur_factor); complex for a real A with complex pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.eigenvalues"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.equilibrate","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"equilibrate(A; radix=false) -> (; matrix, row_scale, column_scale)\n\nRow then column scaling so that the largest entry of every row and column has magnitude 1: matrix = Diagonal(1 ./ row_scale)·A·Diagonal(1 ./ column_scale). radix=true rounds the scales to powers of 2 (scaling without rounding error).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.equilibrate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.expert_cholesky","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"expert_cholesky(A, b; uplo=:L, maxiter=8, rtol=8eps) -> NamedTuple\n\nExpert driver: Cholesky of the Hermitian A (only the uplo triangle is read) then iterative refinement (see refine_solve). Returns (; x, converged, iterations, backward_error, residual_norm, rcond_estimate, forward_error_estimate).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.expert_cholesky"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.expert_solve","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Equilibrated LU expert solve with componentwise backward error and iterative refinement. Returns diagnostics and never silently asserts convergence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.expert_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.expert_symmetric","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"expert_symmetric(A, b; uplo=:L, hermitian=true, maxiter=8, rtol=8eps) -> NamedTuple\n\nExpert driver for symmetric or Hermitian indefinite systems: Bunch–Kaufman LDLᵀ, then iterative refinement. Same result fields as expert_cholesky.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.expert_symmetric"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.frechet_exp","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"frechet_exp(A, E; kwargs...) -> (; value, derivative)\n\nexp(A) and its Fréchet derivative in the direction E, from the upper blocks of exp([A E; 0 A]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.frechet_exp"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.frechet_log","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"frechet_log(A, E; kwargs...) -> (; value, derivative)\n\nlog(A) and its Fréchet derivative in the direction E.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.frechet_log"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.frechet_matrix","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Fréchet derivative L_f(A,E) from the upper block of f([A E;0 A]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.frechet_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.frechet_sqrt","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"frechet_sqrt(A, E; kwargs...) -> (; value, derivative)\n\nsqrt(A) and its Fréchet derivative in the direction E.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.frechet_sqrt"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.generalized_sylvester","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Solve AR-LB=C, DR-LE=F for two (quasi-)triangular pencils. Block substitution with complete pivoting of each 1x1/2x2 coupled system. The result satisfies the scaled right-hand sides: scaleC and scaleF.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.generalized_sylvester"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.hessenberg_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"hessenberg_factor(A)\n\nCompact real/complex Householder reduction A=QHQ'. Neither A nor its views are modified. F.H materializes upper Hessenberg H; F.Q materializes Q. Reflector tails and real tau are retained for implicit application. Input is normalized before reduction to protect finite very large/small matrices. The stored matrix is normalized; F.scale restores the upper Hessenberg entries, not the tails.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hessenberg_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.hessenberg_q","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"hessenberg_q(F::HessenbergFactor; algorithm=:auto, blocksize=32) -> Matrix\n\nForms the unitary Q of a Hessenberg reduction explicitly (blocked application of the reflectors).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.hessenberg_q"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.inverse_norm_estimate","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Hager–Higham estimate of ||A^-1||_1 using native normal/adjoint solves. This is an estimate, not a certified upper bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.inverse_norm_estimate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.khatri_rao","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"khatri_rao(A, B) -> Matrix\n\nColumn-wise Kronecker (Khatri–Rao) product: column j is A[:,j] ⊗ B[:,j].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.khatri_rao"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.kron_product","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"kron_product(A, B) -> Matrix\n\nKronecker product A ⊗ B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.kron_product"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.kron_sum","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"kron_sum(A, B) -> Matrix\n\nKronecker sum A ⊗ I + I ⊗ B of two square matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.kron_sum"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.kronecker_action","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"kronecker_action(A, B, x) -> Vector\n\n(A ⊗ B)·x without forming the Kronecker product: vec(B·X·Aᵀ) with X the reshape of x. Cost O(mn(p+q)) instead of O(mnpq).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.kronecker_action"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.ldlt_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Symmetric/Hermitian indefinite LDL factorization with Bunch–Kaufman 1×1/2×2 diagonal pivot selection. Only the selected input triangle is read. Set pivot=:none for unpivoted LDL. A[p,p]=LD(hermitian ? L' : transpose(L)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ldlt_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.least_squares","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Rank-revealing QR basic least-squares solution (free pivoted variables zero). For a rank-deficient problem this is a basic solution, not the minimum norm solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.least_squares"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.left_null_space","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"left_null_space(A; rtol, atol) -> Matrix\n\nOrthonormal basis of {y : Aᴴ·y = 0}, i.e. null_space(adjoint(A)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.left_null_space"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.logabsdet_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"logabsdet_factor(A) -> (logabs, phase)\nlogabsdet_factor(F::Union{MultifrontalLUFactor, SparseStaticLUFactor, SparseLDLFactor})\n\nlog|det(A)| and the phase det(A)/|det(A)| (the sign for real matrices), from a dense LU or from an existing sparse factorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.logabsdet_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.lq_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Householder LQ: A=L*Q, using native QR of A'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lq_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.lu_complete","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"lu_complete(A; check=true, threaded=true) -> CompleteLUFactor\n\nLU with complete pivoting (A[p,q] = L·U): the most stable LU, at the cost of an O(n²) pivot search per step. Same as lu_factor(A; pivot=:complete).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lu_complete"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.lu_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Partial-pivoted blocked LU. Trailing updates use this package's GEMM.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lu_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.lu_rook","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"lu_rook(A; check=true, threaded=true) -> CompleteLUFactor\n\nLU with rook pivoting (a pivot largest in both its row and its column): nearly as stable as complete pivoting, cheaper on average. Same as lu_factor(A; pivot=:rook).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.lu_rook"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_cos","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"matrix_cos(A; kwargs...) -> Matrix\n\nMatrix cosine, (exp(iA) + exp(-iA))/2; complex result for a real A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_cos"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_exp","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Matrix exponential by degree-13 Padé scaling and squaring (Higham). All products and the rational solve use the native Julia kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\nGeneric matrix exponential using a scaled Taylor series in T. The order is chosen by convergence in the scalar provider's active precision, not a fixed Float64 Padé truncation. Requires +,-,*,/,abs,eps,log2,ceil,ldexp and comparison. Hardware types retain their more specific Padé implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_exp"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_log","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Principal matrix logarithm by complex Schur, inverse scaling/squaring and an atanh series. Singular input is rejected. A finite iteration limit is enforced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_log"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_norm","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"matrix_norm(A, kind=:fro)\n\nMatrix norms: :fro (Frobenius), :one (max column sum), :inf (max row sum), :max (largest entry), :two (largest singular value) and :nuclear (sum of the singular values).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_norm"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_power","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"matrix_power(A, p::Integer; threaded=true)\nmatrix_power(A, p::Number; threaded=true)\n\nA^p. Integer p: repeated squaring (p < 0 through the inverse). Other p: exp(p·log(A)) (principal branch).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_power"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_sign","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Matrix sign via complex Schur. Eigenvalues on the imaginary axis are excluded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_sign"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_sin","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"matrix_sin(A; kwargs...) -> Matrix\n\nMatrix sine, (exp(iA) - exp(-iA))/(2i); complex result for a real A (the imaginary part is rounding noise).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_sin"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.matrix_sqrt","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Principal matrix square root through complex Schur and triangular recurrence. The result is complex even for real input; negative eigenvalues are supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.matrix_sqrt"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.mixed_solve","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Native mixed refinement with Float32 low factors and Float64 residuals. See mixed_solve's extended implementation for diagnostics and family selection.\n\n\n\n\n\nMixed LU/Cholesky/LDL/QR with an explicitly selected low type and generic residuals and updates. Generic inputs preserve their type by default. Reports convergence, metric, low_type and fallback provenance. QR reports a normalized normal residual, not an original-system error bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.mixed_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.native_inverse","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"native_inverse(A; threaded=true) -> Matrix\n\nExplicit inverse through LU (LAPACK getri scheme: inverse of U, then a triangular solve with L, then the permutation). Raises SingularException for a singular A. Solving with a factorization is cheaper and more accurate when only A⁻¹·b is needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.native_inverse"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.null_space","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"null_space(A; rtol, atol) -> Matrix\n\nOrthonormal basis of the null space {x : A·x = 0} (right singular vectors of the singular values below the tolerance, as for numerical_rank).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.null_space"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.numerical_rank","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"numerical_rank(A; rtol=eps·max(m,n), atol=0) -> Int\n\nNumber of singular values above max(atol, rtol·σmax).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.numerical_rank"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.pseudoinverse","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"pseudoinverse(A; rtol=eps·max(m,n), atol=0, algorithm=:golub_kahan) -> Matrix\n\nMoore–Penrose pseudoinverse from the SVD; singular values below max(atol, rtol·σmax) are treated as zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.pseudoinverse"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.ql_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Householder QL: A=Q*L, lower trapezoid anchored at the lower right.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.ql_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.qr_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Compact Householder QR for real/complex rectangular matrices (unpivoted).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qr_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.qr_pivoted","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Column-pivoted Householder QR; A[:,p]=Q*R. Column norms are downdated after each reflector and recomputed when cancellation makes the downdate unreliable, with LAPACK's geqp3/laqp2 criterion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qr_pivoted"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.qr_q","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Materialize selected leading columns of Q without allocating the full Q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qr_q"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.qz_condition","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Pencil diagnostics: homogeneous eigenvalue reciprocal condition numbers, and (when split is given) left/right deflating-subspace projection conditions plus a coupled Sylvester inverse-1-norm separation estimate. No dense n^4 Kronecker operator is formed. Estimates are not certified error bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qz_condition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.qz_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Generalized Schur A=QSZ', B=QTZ'. Real input returns real S with isolated 1x1/2x2 blocks and triangular T; complex input returns triangular S/T. form=:complex explicitly requests the complex path for real input. Homogeneous alpha/beta pairs include infinite and indeterminate eigenvalues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qz_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.qz_reorder","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Reorder whole real 1x1/2x2 or complex 1x1 generalized Schur blocks. The selector receives a generalized eigenvalue (possibly Inf/NaN). Selecting either member of a real pair selects its entire block. Input is not modified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.qz_reorder"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.reciprocal_condition","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"reciprocal_condition(A; maxiter=8)\n\nEstimate of 1/κ₁(A) (LAPACK gecon style: Hager–Higham estimate of ‖A⁻¹‖₁ from an LU factorization). 0 for a singular A. An estimate, not a bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.reciprocal_condition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.refine_solve","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Iterative refinement in the generic work type; Float32 residuals widen to Float64. Returns componentwise backward error, true residual norm and estimated forward error/reciprocal condition. These condition estimates are explicitly non-certified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.refine_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.row_space","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"row_space(A; rtol, atol) -> Matrix\n\nOrthonormal basis of the range of Aᴴ, i.e. column_space(adjoint(A)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.row_space"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.rq_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Householder RQ: A=R*Q, upper trapezoid anchored at the upper right.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.rq_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.schur_condition","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"schur_condition(F::SchurFactor; split=nothing) -> (; eigenvalue, spectral_gap, subspace)\n\nCondition estimates of a Schur factorization (LAPACK trsna/trsen): reciprocal condition numbers of the eigenvalues, separations of the eigenvalues, and of the invariant subspace given by split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.schur_condition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.schur_eigenvectors","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"schur_eigenvectors(F::SchurFactor; left=false) -> Matrix\n\nEigenvectors of the original matrix from its Schur factorization (back substitution on T, then multiplication by Z); left=true gives the left eigenvectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.schur_eigenvectors"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.schur_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"schur_factor(A;maxiter=100*max(1,size(A,1)))\n\nNative Schur decomposition A=ZTZ'. Real inputs use Francis double-shift bulge chasing and return real quasi-triangular T (1x1/2x2 blocks); complex inputs use implicit single-shift QR and triangular T. Eigenvalues are complex. Finite input is scaled before reduction. Failure to converge throws ConvergenceError, including the remaining active block and iteration count. No balancing, reordering or eigenvector back substitution is performed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.schur_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.schur_multishift","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Native real/complex multishift Schur: aggressive early deflation, concurrent small-bulge train, and accumulated blocked far updates. Real input preserves real 1x1/2x2 blocks. workspace reuses panel and update buffers; a shared context is serialized, with reentrant use rejected. No external eigensolver is called.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.schur_multishift"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.schur_reorder","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Stably move selected eigenvalues to the leading complex Schur block. Adjacent unitary swaps preserve A=ZTZ'. Repeated inseparable eigenvalues are rejected if a requested swap cannot distinguish their invariant subspaces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.schur_reorder"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.singular_values","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"singular_values(A; kwargs...) -> Vector\n\nSingular values only, in decreasing order (no vectors computed); keywords as svd_factor (algorithm=:jacobi by default, :golub_kahan, :divide_conquer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.singular_values"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.solve","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"solve(F, b)\n\nSolves A·x = b with a factorization F of A (LUFactor, CholeskyFactor, LDLFactor, QRFactor, PivotedQRFactor, CompleteOrthogonalFactor, AasenFactor, band and packed factors, sparse factors…). b is a vector or a matrix (several right-hand sides) and is not modified. For QR factors the result is the least-squares solution.\n\nF = lu_factor(A); x = solve(F, b)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.solve"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.solve_transposed","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"solve_transposed(F, b; trans=:C)\n\nSolves Aᴴ·x = b (trans=:C) or Aᵀ·x = b (trans=:T) with an LUFactor or CompleteLUFactor of A, without refactorizing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.solve_transposed"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.svd_bidiagonal","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"SVD through two-sided Householder reduction and bidiagonal Golub–Kahan QR. algorithm=:divide_conquer instead solves the symmetric bidiagonal dilation by Cuppen recursion and secular merges; no A'A is formed. Real/complex, tall/wide, values-only and full null-space options are supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_bidiagonal"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.svd_divide_conquer","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"svd_divide_conquer(A; vectors=true, full=false, kwargs...) -> SVDFactor\n\nSVD by bidiagonal reduction and divide-and-conquer (LAPACK gesdd scheme): the fastest full SVD with vectors on large matrices. Same as svd_bidiagonal(A; algorithm=:divide_conquer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_divide_conquer"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.svd_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"svd_factor(A;vectors=true,maxsweeps=100,rtol=8eps(real(eltype(A)))*sqrt(min(size(A)...)))\n\nEconomy SVD A=UDiagonal(S)Vt, sorted descending, for real/complex rectangular matrices, including empty and rank-deficient inputs. Native one-sided cyclic Jacobi; never forms the full Gram matrix. Wide matrices are handled through their adjoints. Column norms and normalized correlations protect graded inputs. Correlated columns at the working type's underflow floor are deflated to zero; orthogonal tiny columns are retained. vectors=false returns U=Vt=nothing. Nonconvergence throws ConvergenceError. full=true completes the null spaces. algorithm=:parallel_jacobi selects disjoint column rounds; :golub_kahan and :divide_conquer use the bidiagonal entry point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.svd_randomized","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Randomized range-finder SVD with explicit local seed and reorthogonalized power steps. Intended for low target rank; residual quality is caller-controlled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_randomized"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.svd_selected","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Genuine selected dense SVD, with 1-based descending indices, or the half-open value interval (low,high]. Only the requested distinct ranks are bisected and only their vectors are computed/backtransformed. Index order and duplicates are preserved. With neither selector, all min(m,n) values are requested.\n\nrepresentation=:dilation uses the symmetric bidiagonal linearization, avoiding B'B's squared conditioning. :gram ports Rust's tridiagonal B'B representation explicitly. No external solver or full-spectrum safety fallback is used. diagnostics=Ref{Any}() receives work counts, without changing SVDFactor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_selected"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.svd_truncated","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Leading-rank SVD. The default uses genuine selected bisection/inverse iteration. Explicit :golubkahan/:divideconquer retain the full-spectrum paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.svd_truncated"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.trevc_left","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"trevc_left(S; packed=T<:Real) -> Matrix\n\nLeft eigenvectors of a matrix in Schur form S (LAPACK trevc), the counterpart of trevc_right.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trevc_left"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.trevc_right","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Native block TREVC. Real input stores a conjugate pair as real/imaginary columns, as in OxiBLAS Rust; complex input returns complex eigenvectors. packed=false returns one complex vector per eigenvalue for real input too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trevc_right"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.tridiagonal_factor","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"tridiagonal_factor(A;uplo=:U)\n\nReduce a symmetric/Hermitian matrix to a real symmetric tridiagonal: A=QTQ'. Only the chosen triangle and real diagonal are read. The compact Householder representation retains the diagonal phase correction needed for complex eigenvectors. F.diagonal/F.offdiagonal are in input units; the fields diag/offdiag are normalized by F.reflectors.scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tridiagonal_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.tridiagonal_q","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"tridiagonal_q(F::TridiagonalFactor; algorithm=:auto, blocksize=32) -> Matrix\n\nForms the unitary Q of a tridiagonal reduction explicitly, including the phases that make the off-diagonal real.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.tridiagonal_q"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.trsna_s","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Reciprocal eigenvalue condition numbers |y'x|/(norm(y)norm(x)). Ports Rust TRSNA-S, including one common value for a real conjugate pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trsna_s"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lapack/#OxiBLAS.trsna_sep","page":"Dense factorizations and LAPACK","text":"Rust TRSNA-SEP's spectral-distance estimate (not a singular-value sep). Repeated eigenvalues return zero; an isolated block has no complement and returns Inf. Use schur_condition for the coupled invariant-subspace estimate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OxiBLAS.trsna_sep"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/spectral/#Spectral-and-iterative-algorithms","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"The dense spectral decompositions (Schur, Hermitian, SVD, QZ) are described in Dense factorizations. This page covers the iterative and partial methods, usable on dense or sparse matrices.\n\nusing OxiBLAS, SparseArrays, LinearAlgebra\nn = 200\nL = spdiagm(-1 => -ones(n-1), 0 => 2ones(n), 1 => -ones(n-1))   # 1-D Laplacian\nr = lobpcg(CSRMatrix(L); nev=3, which=:smallest)\n(r.converged, r.values)\n(false, [0.00024455644757117353, 0.0009785132188212828, 0.0023231507256374828])","title":"Spectral and iterative algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/spectral/#Eigenvalues","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Function Method\nlanczos(A; nev, which), thick_restart_lanczos Hermitian Lanczos, thick restart\narnoldi, iram, thick_restart_arnoldi Arnoldi, implicit or thick restart\nblock_lanczos, block_arnoldi (and *_factor) block variants\nlobpcg(A; nev, B, which) LOBPCG, generalized problems possible\nshift_invert spectral transformation\npolynomial_filter, apply_filter!, filter_response Chebyshev filters (ChebyshevFilter, FilterWorkspace)\neigenvalues_in_interval, count_eigenvalues_in_interval, polynomial_filtered_eigenvalues, hermitian_spectral_bounds interval search","title":"Eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/spectral/#Singular-values","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"Function Method\nsparse_svd_lanczos, sparse_svd partial sparse SVD (Golub–Kahan by default), SparseSVDWorkspace\nsvd_selected(A; indices, interval), svd_truncated, svd_randomized partial dense SVDs\nincremental_svd, svd_add_rows!, svd_add_columns!, svd_snapshot, svd_rank SVD updated by rows or columns (IncrementalSVDFactor)","title":"Singular values"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/spectral/#Stochastic-estimators","page":"Spectral and iterative algorithms","text":"trace_hutchinson, trace_hutch_plus_plus, trace_xtrace and trace_xtrace_loo estimate the trace. stochastic_diagonal, stochastic_frobenius and stochastic_logdet estimate the diagonal, the Frobenius norm and the log-determinant. All are configured by StochasticConfig(; num_probes, seed, ...), with an explicit seed and no global state.","title":"Stochastic estimators"}]
}
